.PP
In the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood, expand_aliases is on in an interactive
shell.
Every other option has the same default in every mood.
The moods also differ in grammar, expansion, builtins, and errors, as
described under Shell grammar, Expansions, Builtins, and Shell errors.
.SS Options
The options and configuration reference lists command\-line flags and
shell settings, including their aliases and restrictions.
.PP
An option given on the command line, on or off, overrides the settings
files and \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R].
.SS Diagnostics
A diagnostic is written to standard error.
It begins with the file, line, and column, then \f[CR]error\f[R],
\f[CR]warning\f[R], or \f[CR]note\f[R], and the message, followed by the
source line and a caret under the column.
A finding of analysis ends with its check code in parentheses, such as
(SC2086), and a \f[CR]note:\f[R] line may suggest a fix.
.PP
Text built at run time is its own source, so a location in an eval
string appears as \f[CR]eval:2:7:\f[R], in a trap action as
\f[CR]trap:1:5:\f[R], and in a \f[CR]\-c\f[R] string as
\f[CR]\-c:1:5:\f[R].
Standard input and interactive lines have no label.
.PP
Each function call, sourced file, eval, trap action, subshell, and
command, function, or process substitution between the top level and the
failing command adds a backtrace frame beginning with \f[CR]trace:\f[R],
and a repeated frame is printed once with a repeat count.
With several \f[CR]\-c\f[R] strings or files, the frame pointing into
the command line is printed once after the diagnostics of that string or
file.
.PP
Diagnostics read the same in every mood.
On a color terminal the labels and codes are colored.
.PP
Linting, the language server, and script diagnostics report every syntax
error in one run.
A construct unavailable in the selected mood, such as \f[CR][[\f[R] in
the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, is reported, and its contents are checked for
further syntax errors.
.PP
Analysis checks a script before it runs.
It has strict, lenient, and annoying tiers, which \f[CR]\-W\f[R] selects
as described under command\-line options, and
\f[CR]\-\-list\-diagnostics\f[R] lists every check.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood analysis runs by default, and strict and
lenient findings reject the script, which then exits with status 1
without running.
.PP
They include the following cases.
.IP \[bu] 2
A variable is expanded but never assigned.
.IP \[bu] 2
A function that uses arguments is called without arguments.
.IP \[bu] 2
A function is called before its definition.
.PP
An assignment in a function to a variable not declared \f[CR]local\f[R]
is reported.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, which has no \f[CR]local\f[R], only a
variable first set in a function and also used outside it is reported.
.PP
In the other moods analysis runs only at a level that \f[CR]\-W\f[R] or
\f[CR]set \-W\f[R] selects, and its findings are warnings.
An interactive shell analyzes each line before it runs it.
A script operand without a shebang is reported when the level is not 0.
.PP
Analysis follows static source operands into readable files.
It checks a command, backtick, or process substitution as a subshell,
and a function substitution in the current shell.
When more than one finding is reported, a summary such as
\f[CR]Encountered 1 warning and 2 errors.\f[R] follows them.
.PP
A comment such as
\f[CR]# shellcheck disable=SC2086,unquoted\-command\-substitution\f[R]
suppresses analysis checks.
A numeric code suppresses every variant under it, a slug only its own
variant, and the value may be \f[CR]all\f[R] or a range such as
\f[CR]SC2000\-SC2100\f[R].
Before the first command it covers the file, and later it covers the
next and\-or command.
It does not suppress parser or runtime diagnostics.
.PP
An unavailable command or path is reported through its first unavailable
component when that can be identified.
An internal error stops the shell with an \f[CR]error:\f[R] line and
\f[CR]note:\f[R] lines identifying the process and build, and the shell
aborts.
.SS Linting
\f[CR]kosh \-\-lint\f[R] analyzes shell source without running it, with
every diagnostic tier enabled at the severity of the mood, regardless of
\f[CR]\-W\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-no\-diagnostics\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics\f[R].
Each command string is analyzed in order, then each file operand, or
standard input when neither is given.
Startup and settings files are skipped.
The diagnostics go to standard error, and standard output stays empty.
.PP
Shell commands are also extracted from CI configurations, Ansible,
Dockerfile, Compose, Markdown, Makefile, justfile, Kubernetes, and
similar hosts, and only the extracted sh, Bash, or kosh source is
checked.
A file counts as shell source by its extension or shebang, and a file in
no recognized format fails with status 1 and a list of the formats.
.PP
A host interpreter setting, such as a step shell, a SHELL assignment or
instruction, a shebang, or an executable option, selects the shell, and
a command for another interpreter is skipped.
An exec array contributes only the script of a shell \f[CR]\-c\f[R]
command.
A justfile recipe line, joined across backslash continuations, is one
command, and a shebang or script recipe is one script.
.PP
A Jinja template of a shell script, such as \f[I]name\f[R].sh.j2, is
checked with its template syntax masked.
Host template expressions and editor variables are masked in every
format, interpolated values count as plain words, and an unresolved
command is not reported in a fragment that holds an interpolation.
.PP
Variables that the host provides, such as CI variables, exported recipe
parameters, and Dockerfile ARG and ENV values, count as set.
Diagnostics point into the host file.
.PP
With \f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R], safe structured fixes are written to each
file, a summary of the fixes is printed, and only the diagnostics that
remain are reported.
A fixed file that would no longer parse is left unchanged.
A warning\-only result exits with status 0, and a remaining error or an
input or write failure exits with status 1.
.SS Formatting
\f[CR]kosh \-\-format\f[R] formats shell source without running it.
Structural keywords and compound braces go on separate lines, each
nested level indents by two spaces, direct \f[CR]test\f[R] commands
become bracket commands unless an earlier alias or function shadows
them, code wraps at 80 columns at a safe token boundary, and lines end
in LF.
.PP
With no file or a lone \f[CR]\-\f[R], standard input is formatted, and
otherwise one file is formatted to standard output, highlighted when it
is a terminal.
In a host format, only the shell fragments that the host allows to
change are formatted, such as Markdown code blocks.
A syntax error is reported and exits with status 1.
Combined with \f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R], the lint fixes are applied first.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R] replaces each file operand with the result.
A changed file is replaced atomically through a temporary file beside
it, and its mode and symbolic links are kept.
A file that is not a regular file, or one that changed while it was
processed, is refused.
.SS Language server
\f[CR]kosh \-\-as\-language\-server\f[R] runs a language server over
standard input and output for editor integration.
It reports diagnostics after each document change and provides
completion, quick fixes, a fix\-all action for non\-conflicting safe
fixes, formatting, definition lookup, hover information, semantic
highlighting, a document outline, and rename.
Completion is triggered by a space or a dash.
.PP
The server works inside the shell regions of the host formats that
\f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R] recognizes and follows relative sources from the
document directory.
Rename updates variables, functions, and aliases in the open document
and keeps the sigil and braces of \f[CR]$name\f[R] and
\f[CR]${name}\f[R].
A command can be renamed only when the document defines it as a function
or alias.
.PP
The mood of a document comes from a recognized shebang, then from a
language identifier of kosh, bash, sh, or bash\-posix, with rbash and
shellscript meaning bash, then from the extension .kosh, .bash, .sh, or
\&.dash, and otherwise it is \f[CR]kosh\f[R].
The server skips startup and settings files, and it exits with status 0
after a shutdown request and 1 otherwise.
.SS Shell settings
Shell settings can be read and changed through \f[CR]set\f[R],
\f[CR]shopt\f[R], and \f[CR]koshconf\f[R].
See the settings reference for option names, ids, scopes, and value
restrictions.
.SS Startup files
Every shell applies the settings files before any startup file.
They are the system file /etc/kosh.conf
(%ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]kosh.conf on Windows), then the user file
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf or \[ti]/.config/kosh/kosh.conf, then a
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] variable.
The shell removes KOSHCONF from the environment after it applies it.
.PP
These files hold settings, and Configuration files describes them.
A mood set there selects the startup files below unless the command line
or the invocation name selects one, and options given on the command
line keep their values.
The superuser on POSIX has no user settings file and no rc script.
.PP
A login or interactive shell in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood sources the rc
script, $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh or
\[ti]/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh, and no system rc file.
In an interactive non\-login shell, \f[CR]\-\-norc\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-rcfile\f[R], and privileged mode apply to it as they apply to
\[ti]/.bashrc.
A Bash startup file that the script sources runs in the \f[CR]bash\f[R]
mood, as described under \f[CR]source\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]\-L\f[R] on the command line, or init_moods in a settings file,
replaces the list of moods whose startup files run, so
\f[CR]\-L bash\f[R] makes a \f[CR]kosh\f[R] session source only the Bash
startup files, in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood, and then return to the
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, and \f[CR]\-L bash,kosh\f[R] also sources the rc
script afterwards.
.PP
The compatibility moods use the files described under Bash and POSIX
startup files.
.SS Skipping startup files
Clean (\f[CR]\-Q\f[R]), rescue, lint, format, and language server shells
skip every startup and settings file, as does a shell whose real and
effective ids differ.
\f[CR]\-\-no\-config\f[R] and privileged mode skip the settings files
and KOSHCONF.
A subshell receives its settings from its parent, and an executable
started as a bundled utility or as koshkit applies none.
.PP
Privileged mode suppresses ENV, BASH_ENV, and the rc script of an
interactive non\-login \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood shell.
Restricted mode, nounset, pipefail, and analysis take effect after
startup, but set, shopt, and koshconf in a startup file already reject a
value that the mood does not hold.
A missing startup file is skipped, and an unreadable custom rc or
BASH_ENV file is also reported.
.SS Line editing
The interactive editor starts in emacs mode.
Use \f[CR]set \-o vi\f[R] to select vi mode and \f[CR]set \-o emacs\f[R]
to return.
The prompts are described under Prompting.
Input taller than the terminal shows the rows around the caret and
leaves two rows for hints, without the ghost suggestion and right
prompt.
.PP
A control character is drawn in caret notation, such as \[ha][ for ESC,
and a filename holding one completes in the $\[aq]...\[aq] form.
Bracketed paste inserts text without running it.
.PP
Kill commands save the erased text in a 16\-entry kill ring for the
session, and consecutive kills join into one entry.
Ctrl\-K on an empty line joins the next line, and on an empty last line
removes it.
Undo reverts a yank or a last\-word insertion with all its replacements
in one step.
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E, or \f[CR]:e\f[R] in vi mode, opens the line in VISUAL,
EDITOR, or vi; when the editor exits with status 0 the saved text
replaces the line without running it, and otherwise the line is
unchanged and a message appears.
.PP
Ctrl\-Z undoes while the editor collects a line, and stops a foreground
program otherwise.
Ctrl\-Shift\-Z redoes when the terminal reports it apart from Ctrl\-Z;
the editor requests such reports through the kitty keyboard protocol and
xterm modifyOtherKeys, which tmux passes on with its
\f[CR]extended\-keys\f[R] option on.
\f[CR]koshconf set editor.request_extended_key_reports off\f[R] stops
the requests.
On the Windows console both keys undo and Ctrl\-\[ha] redoes.
.PP
On a non\-empty line, Up recalls only history entries that begin with
the text typed before the first Up, and Down moves back; an empty line
steps through all history.
editor.history.arrow_keys_search_by_typed_prefix, on by default,
controls this.
\f[CR]koshconf set editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes on\f[R] makes a
typed (, [, {, or quote insert its closer, step over a typed closer, and
delete an empty pair on Backspace.
.PP
When the caret is on or just after a bracket, the bracket and its
partner are highlighted; a bracket inside quotes, a comment, a
here\-document, or escaped never pairs.
.SS Hints
Once the caret passes the command word, hints appear below the input
under a \f[CR]synopsis\f[R] heading.
Up to 16 rows explain the command, its subcommand, and its flags.
The current word is marked with \f[CR]>\f[R].
Hints come from manual pages, builtin or koshkit help, alias
definitions, and function locations.
.PP
After a key prefix such as Ctrl\-X or a vi operator, the hints list the
keys that complete it.
Hints are hidden while a menu or search is open.
.PP
After a short pause, the hint of an external program loads its section 1
manual page, a subcommand page such as \f[CR]git\-commit\f[R](1), or,
for an allowlisted command, its help output.
.PP
\f[CR]man\f[R], \f[CR]manpath\f[R], and the program run only from an
absolute directory owned by the user or root that no group or other user
can write, and never through an empty or relative PATH element.
A keystroke never runs the command itself.
A Tab that needs a load shows \f[CR]loading...\f[R] while it waits.
.PP
After the same pause, the first diagnostic for the current input appears
as \f[CR]error\f[R], \f[CR]warning\f[R], or \f[CR]note\f[R].
This check does not follow sourced files, look up commands, or open
files.
If the input cannot be parsed, its first error appears under
\f[CR]syntax error\f[R].
Enter submits the whole buffer without a continuation prompt.
An unfinished construct is therefore reported as an error.
.PP
Set editor.hints.show_command_synopsis to off to hide command
explanations and stop their documentation loads.
Set editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics to off to hide findings and
syntax errors.
Both options are on by default.
Turning off both also hides the pending\-key rows.
.SS Completion
Ghost text offers prefix matches from completion and history; the right
arrow or End at the end of the line accepts it, and Ctrl\-Right or
Alt\-F its next word.
.PP
Tab also offers smart\-case and subsequence matches.
.IP \[bu] 2
An empty command position offers keywords, builtins, functions, aliases,
bundled utilities, and PATH programs.
.IP \[bu] 2
Other words complete as paths.
.IP \[bu] 2
A word beginning with $ or ${ completes as a variable.
.IP \[bu] 2
A word beginning with \[ti] completes as a user.
.IP \[bu] 2
A word beginning with a dash completes as a flag of the command.
.PP
A spec registered with \f[CR]complete\f[R] takes precedence for its
command.
A directory candidate ends in a slash.
The operand of \f[CR]cd\f[R] or \f[CR]pushd\f[R] completes directories,
also under each CDPATH entry unless it is absolute or starts with a dot
or tilde.
Targets of \f[CR]make\f[R] come from the Makefile, and those after
\f[CR]cmake \-\-build\f[R] \f[I]dir\f[R] \f[CR]\-\-target\f[R] from
\f[I]dir\f[R]/CMakeFiles/TargetDirectories.txt, without running either
program or any $(shell ...)
function.
.PP
An explicit Tab first inserts the longest common prefix.
A sole candidate replaces the token, followed by a space as
completion.add_space_after_completed_word selects: \f[CR]off\f[R] (the
default), \f[CR]on\f[R], or \f[CR]on\-excluding\-trailing\-slash\f[R].
Several candidates go to the selector of \f[CR]\-\-tab\-selector\f[R] or
completion.menu_style.
.PP
The interactive selector draws a menu below the line, headed
\f[CR]selecting completions\f[R] or, for Ctrl\-R,
\f[CR]incremental history search\f[R].
.PP
Typing narrows the menu: command, path, and koshconf option lists keep
the best of the exact\-prefix, smart\-case\-prefix, and subsequence
tiers, and other lists keep every case\-insensitive prefix match.
.PP
A history entry matches when it begins with the query, contains the
query, or contains the query\[aq]s bytes in order.
.PP
Backspace widens the list.
Typing a blank or slash, or erasing past the start of the token,
refreshes the candidates after a pause.
Accepting a directory opens a menu of its entries.
.PP
The external selector passes the candidates to
\f[CR]KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND\f[R] and replaces the token with its
output.
Dismissing the selector leaves the line unchanged.
A single candidate or a session without a controlling terminal uses the
plain listing.
An empty selector command, a command that cannot be found, or an exit
status other than 0, 1, or 130 also uses the plain listing after a
message.
.PP
The plain selector prints the candidates as a column list, which a
terminal without decoration support always receives; for Ctrl\-R it
keeps the incremental search inside the editor.
.PP
Ctrl\-R searches the history through the selected selector, newest
first, with the line as the query; an accepted entry replaces the line.
The search uses a snapshot of the shared history file and does not
change the private history.
.SS Terminal integration
An interactive shell with terminal output writes OSC 133 A, B, C, and D
marks and OSC 7 working directory marks, plus OSC 633 marks when
TERM_PROGRAM is vscode, so terminals can find prompts, commands,
statuses, and the directory.
No mark is written when TERM is dumb or
\f[CR]KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION\f[R] is 0.
.SS Emacs mode keys
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-A, Home\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to the start of the line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-E, End\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to the end of the line, or accept the ghost suggestion at the end
of the buffer.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-Home, Ctrl\-End, Ctrl\-Up, Ctrl\-Down, Ctrl\-PageUp\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to the start or end of the buffer.
Ctrl\-PageUp moves to the start.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]PageUp, PageDown\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move the cursor up or down by the rows of the screen.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-B, Left\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move one character left.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Right\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move one character right, or accept the ghost suggestion at the end of
the buffer.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-B, Ctrl\-Left, Alt\-Left\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to the start of the previous word.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-F, Ctrl\-Right, Alt\-Right\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move past the next word, or accept the next word of the ghost
suggestion.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-P, Up\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move up a row, or recall an older entry from the first row.
A typed or edited multi\-line buffer that joins no line with a backslash
needs a second press.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-N, Down\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move down a row, or recall a newer entry from the last row.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-<, Alt\-,, Alt\->\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Recall the oldest or newest entry, or in \f[CR]slurp\f[R] move to the
start or end of the file.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Enter, Ctrl\-J, Ctrl\-M, Shift\-Enter\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run the line, or continue it after a trailing backslash.
A typed or edited multi\-line buffer that joins no line with a backslash
runs on a second press.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-Enter\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Insert a line break.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Tab, Shift\-Tab, Ctrl\-Space\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Complete the token.
Ctrl\-Space does so only on POSIX.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Backspace, Ctrl\-H, Delete\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Delete the character before or under the cursor.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-D\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Delete the character under the cursor, or exit on an empty line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-W, Alt\-Backspace, Alt\-H\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Kill the previous word.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-D, Ctrl\-Delete\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Kill to the next word boundary, which is the rest of the word under the
cursor, or the blanks before the next word.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-K, Ctrl\-U\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Kill to the end or start of the line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-Y, Alt\-Y\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Yank the newest kill, or replace the yank with the next older kill.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-T, Alt\-T\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Swap the characters or words around the cursor.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-U, Alt\-L, Alt\-C\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Uppercase, lowercase, or capitalize to the end of the word.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-\[rs]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Delete blanks around the cursor.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-., Alt\-_\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Insert the last word of an older history entry on each press.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-_, Ctrl\-Z, Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-U\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Undo.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-\[ha], Ctrl\-Shift\-Z\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Redo.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Edit the line in an external editor.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Alt\-X, Ctrl\-X z\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Open the M\-x minibuffer, or repeat the last M\-x command.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Exit with the status of the last command.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-X s, Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-S\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Save the file in \f[CR]slurp\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-S\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Find text in the buffer as it is typed, forward and wrapping around;
text without capitals matches either case.
Ctrl\-S and Ctrl\-R move to the next and previous match, and on empty
text reuse the last search.
Enter keeps the cursor there, and Escape returns it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-R, Ctrl\-F\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Search the history.
In \f[CR]slurp\f[R], Ctrl\-R finds text backward.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-L\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Clear the screen.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-C\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Abandon the line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-V\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Enter multi\-cursor mode.
.PP
Shift or Alt with Up, Down, Left, Right, Home, or End acts as the key
alone, except where an entry above gives it a meaning, and
Ctrl\-Backspace deletes one character.
.SS Vi insert mode keys
Every emacs key works except Ctrl\-V, Alt\-X, and the Ctrl\-X chords.
Arrow keys recall history.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Escape\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Enter command mode and move one character left.
.SS Vi command mode keys
In command mode the arrow keys move between rows without recalling
history.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]1\-9, then 0\-9\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Count for the next command.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[dq]x\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Use register \f[I]x\f[R] (a to z, either case) for the next delete,
change, yank, or paste.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]h, l, Left, Right, Backspace, Space, Ctrl\-B, Ctrl\-F\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move left or right.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]w, W, b, B, e, E\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to the next start, previous start, or end of a word or
blank\-separated word.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]0, \[ha], $, Home, End, Ctrl\-A, Ctrl\-E\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to the line start, first non\-blank, or line end.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]fx, Fx, tx, Tx, ;, ,\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Find \f[I]x\f[R] forward or backward, stop before it, or repeat the
find.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]k, j, Up, Down, Ctrl\-P, Ctrl\-N\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move between rows; k and j recall history from the first or last row.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]gg, G\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to the first or last row, or row N with a count.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]d, c, y\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Delete, change, or yank over a motion; doubled, or with j, k, Up, or
Down, over whole lines.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]gq, gqq\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Wrap the lines of a motion, or the current line, at editor.wrap_width
(80), keeping the indentation of each paragraph\[aq]s first line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]x, X, D, C, s, S\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Delete under or before the cursor, delete or change to the line end,
change one character, or change the line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]rx, \[ti]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Replace the character, or toggle its case and move right.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]p, P\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Paste after or before the cursor.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]o, O, J\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Open a line below or above, or join the next line with one space.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]{, }\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to the previous or next empty line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]i, a, I, A, R\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Insert before or after the cursor, at the first non\-blank, or at the
line end; R inserts without overwriting.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB].\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Repeat the last change other than a yank.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]u, Ctrl\-_, Ctrl\-Z\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Undo.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-R, Ctrl\-\[ha], Ctrl\-Shift\-Z\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Redo.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]/, ?, n, N\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Search the history, or in \f[CR]slurp\f[R] find text forward or backward
and repeat the find in either direction.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Open the colon command line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]v, V, Ctrl\-V\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Select characters, lines, or a rectangle.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Enter, Ctrl\-D, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-L\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Act as in emacs mode.
.SS Vi visual and rectangle keys
Motions move the end of the selection, or the rectangle corner, without
a count.
Visual mode accepts \f[CR]d\f[R], \f[CR]x\f[R] (delete), \f[CR]c\f[R]
(change), \f[CR]y\f[R] (yank), \f[CR]gq\f[R] (wrap, repeatable with .),
and \f[CR]:\f[R] (colon commands on the selected lines, shown as
:\[aq]<,\[aq]>).
Rectangle mode accepts \f[CR]d\f[R], \f[CR]x\f[R] (delete), and
\f[CR]I\f[R], \f[CR]c\f[R], \f[CR]C\f[R] (insert at the left column of
every line when insert mode ends).
Escape, \f[CR]v\f[R] or Ctrl\-V, and any other unrecognized key leave
the selection, and Enter, Ctrl\-D, and Ctrl\-C leave it and act as in
emacs mode.
.SS Vi colon commands
Enter runs the command, and Escape, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D, or Backspace on an
empty line closes it.
Unknown commands do nothing.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:q, :q!, :quit, :wq, :wq!, :wqa, :wqa!, :x\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Exit with the status of the last command.
In \f[CR]slurp\f[R], the w forms save first.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:N, :$\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move to row N of the buffer, or to the last row.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:w, :write\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Save the file in \f[CR]slurp\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:s/old/new/[g]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Replace the first match of the basic regular expression \f[I]old\f[R] on
the caret row, or every match with \f[CR]g\f[R]; \f[CR]:%s\f[R] acts on
every row.
Any punctuation can delimit, and in \f[I]new\f[R] \f[CR]&\f[R] is the
match and \f[CR]\[rs]1\f[R]\-\f[CR]\[rs]9\f[R] its groups.
\f[CR]:&\f[R], \f[CR]:&&\f[R], and \f[CR]:s\f[R] repeat the last
substitution, \f[CR]:&&\f[R] with its \f[CR]g\f[R] flag, and
\f[CR]:%&\f[R] and \f[CR]:%&&\f[R] on every row.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:set option[=value|?], :set nooption\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Set, show, or turn off a \f[CR]koshconf\f[R] option; Tab completes it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:save [option]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Write the option, or every option, to \f[I]kosh.conf\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:format, :%format\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Format the caret row or the buffer as \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R] does; a
source that does not parse is kept and its error shown.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:e, :edit, :u, :undo, :red, :redo\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Edit in an external editor, undo, or redo.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:h, :help\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
List the colon commands until the next key.
.SS Emacs M\-x commands
Tab completes a command, Enter runs it, and Escape, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D, or
Backspace on an empty minibuffer closes it.
The hint rows list the matching commands, each with what it does when
six or fewer match.
The commands are \f[CR]help\f[R] (list the commands),
\f[CR]replace\-string\f[R] and \f[CR]replace\-regexp\f[R] (replace every
match in the buffer, with \[rs]& and \[rs]1\-\[rs]9 in a regexp
replacement), \f[CR]repeat\f[R] (as Ctrl\-X z), \f[CR]set\-option\f[R],
\f[CR]get\-option\f[R], and \f[CR]save\-option\f[R] (as :set and :save),
\f[CR]format\f[R] and \f[CR]format\-buffer\f[R] (as :format and
:%format), \f[CR]fill\-paragraph\f[R] (as gq on the paragraph),
\f[CR]isearch\-forward\f[R] and \f[CR]isearch\-backward\f[R],
\f[CR]kill\-kosh\f[R] (as Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C), \f[CR]undo\f[R] and
\f[CR]undo\-redo\f[R], and the commands of the matching keys:
\f[CR]beginning\-of\-line\f[R], \f[CR]end\-of\-line\f[R],
\f[CR]forward\-word\f[R], \f[CR]backward\-word\f[R],
\f[CR]scroll\-down\-command\f[R], \f[CR]scroll\-up\-command\f[R],
\f[CR]beginning\-of\-history\f[R], \f[CR]end\-of\-history\f[R],
\f[CR]delete\-char\f[R], \f[CR]backward\-delete\-char\f[R],
\f[CR]kill\-word\f[R], \f[CR]backward\-kill\-word\f[R],
\f[CR]kill\-line\f[R], \f[CR]unix\-line\-discard\f[R], \f[CR]yank\f[R],
\f[CR]yank\-last\-arg\f[R], \f[CR]transpose\-chars\f[R],
\f[CR]transpose\-words\f[R], \f[CR]upcase\-word\f[R],
\f[CR]downcase\-word\f[R], \f[CR]capitalize\-word\f[R],
\f[CR]delete\-horizontal\-space\f[R], and \f[CR]clear\-screen\f[R].
.SS Multi\-cursor keys
Up, Down, Ctrl\-P, and Ctrl\-N add a cursor on the row above or below.
Left, Right, Home, End, Ctrl\-B, Ctrl\-F, Ctrl\-A, and Ctrl\-E move
every cursor, and typed characters and Backspace act at every cursor.
Ctrl\-G, Escape, and any other unrecognized key leave the mode, and
Enter, Ctrl\-D, and Ctrl\-C leave it and act as in emacs mode.
.SS Completion menu keys
\f[B]\f[CB]Down, Ctrl\-N, Up, Ctrl\-P, Shift\-Tab\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Highlight the next or previous row, wrapping around.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Tab\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Accept the row, opening a directory.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Right\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Accept the row and offer the completions that follow it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Enter\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Close the menu and run the line as it stands.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]character, Backspace\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Narrow or widen the rows.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Escape\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Close the menu and keep the edited line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-G\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Close the menu and restore the original line, as does any other
unrecognized key.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]other\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Close the menu, then act on the line.
.SS History search keys
In the interactive selector, Enter and Tab accept the entry without
running it, Escape, Ctrl\-G, and other unrecognized keys restore the
line, and the other keys act as in the completion menu.
The plain selector accepts:
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Down, Ctrl\-N, Ctrl\-R\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Step to an older match.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Up, Ctrl\-P, Ctrl\-F, Shift\-Tab\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Step to a newer match.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]character, Backspace\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Extend or shorten the query.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Enter, Tab\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Accept the match without running it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Escape, Ctrl\-G\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Cancel and restore the line, as does any other unrecognized key.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Cancel, restore the line, and act on it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]other\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Accept the match, then act on the line.
.SS Prompting
An interactive shell prints PS1 before it reads a command.
PS0 is printed after a command is read and before it runs.
PROMPT_COMMAND runs before each prompt.
It is a command or, as an indexed array, a list of commands, which run
in order.
Each sees the status and $_ of the previous command, and the hook does
not change them.
.PP
PS2 is \[dq]> \[dq] by default, but the editor submits the whole buffer
on Enter with no continuation prompt.
PS3 is the prompt of \f[CR]select\f[R].
PS4 is printed before each line of \f[CR]xtrace\f[R] output, \[dq]+
\[dq] by default, and its first byte repeats once per enclosing
substitution, eval, source, and trap action.
.PP
PS0, PS1, PS2, and the right prompt undergo parameter expansion, command
substitution, and arithmetic expansion each time they are printed, after
the backslash escapes below are replaced.
PS4 is printed as written.
The default PS1 shows the git branch with ahead and behind counts, the
user, host, and directory, and a bullet.
.PP
RPS1, or RPROMPT when RPS1 is unset or empty, is a right prompt drawn on
the first input row.
It is expanded like PS1, blanks at either end are dropped, and it is
hidden when there is no room or when it holds a control character other
than a color sequence.
An interactive shell started with neither set uses a dim \[dq]\[rs]L
\[rs]M\[dq].
.PP
A nonempty KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1 replaces PS1 on a line that Enter submits
or Ctrl\-C interrupts, so KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1=\[aq]\[rs]$ \[aq] leaves
\[dq]$ \[dq] before each command in the scrollback.
KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1 does the same for the right prompt.
An interactive shell started without it sets it to \[rs][\[rs]], which
erases the right prompt, and KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1=\[aq]\[rs]L\[aq] keeps
each duration.
.PP
The prompts recognize these escapes.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]u, \[rs]h, \[rs]H\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The user, the host up to the first dot, and the full host.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]k, \[rs]K\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\[rs]h and \[rs]H, colored on a color terminal.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]w, \[rs]W, \[rs]P\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The working directory with HOME shown as \[ti], its last component, and
the \[rs]w form shortened to ...
and its last 21 bytes when it is longer than 24 bytes.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]g\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The git branch, as in KOSH_GIT_BRANCH.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]$, \[rs]?\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
# for the superuser and $ otherwise, and the status of the last command.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]j\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The number of jobs.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]L, \[rs]M\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The duration of the last command, empty under 5 milliseconds, and the
resident memory it used.
\[rs]M is the highest peak of its processes and their descendants, or
the growth of the shell during the command if larger, and it is empty
when the shell only reused memory.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs].\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A bullet colored by the last status.
It is white after 0, bold red after 1, and on a truecolor terminal it
shifts toward magenta in four steps for higher statuses.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]t, \[rs]T, \[rs]\[at], \[rs]A, \[rs]d\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The time as HH:MM:SS in 24\-hour and 12\-hour form, the 12\-hour time as
HH:MM with AM or PM, the time as HH:MM, and the date as weekday, month,
and day.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]D{format}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The time formatted by strftime, or as HH:MM:SS for an empty format.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]s, \[rs]v, \[rs]V\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The basename of the invocation path without a login dash or .exe, the
major and minor version, and the full version.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]!, \[rs]#\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The history number of the next command, and the command number, counting
from 1 for each line read from a terminal, script, or standard input.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]l\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The last component of the terminal of standard input, or tty.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]n, \[rs]r, \[rs]e, \[rs]a, \[rs]\[rs]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A newline, a carriage return, an escape, a bell, and a backslash.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs]nnn\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The byte with octal value nnn, of one to three digits.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[rs][, \[rs]]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Begin and end a region that takes no space on the screen, such as a
color sequence.
A raw 001 or 002 byte in PS0, PS1, RPS1, KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1, or
KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1 does the same.
.PP
Any other backslash sequence is printed as written.
Text inserted by an escape is never expanded, and the user, host,
directory, branch, shell, and version draw a control byte in caret
notation and a byte that is not UTF\-8 as \[rs]xHH.
The variables KOSH_PS1_USER (\[rs]u), KOSH_PS1_HOST (\[rs]h),
KOSH_PS1_HOST_FULL (\[rs]H), KOSH_PS1_CWD (\[rs]w), KOSH_PS1_CWD_BASE
(\[rs]W), KOSH_PS1_CWD_SHORT (\[rs]P), KOSH_PS1_GIT_BRANCH (\[rs]g),
KOSH_PS1_PROMPT_SIGN (\[rs]$), KOSH_PS1_STATUS (\[rs]?), KOSH_PS1_JOBS
(\[rs]j), KOSH_PS1_DURATION (\[rs]L), KOSH_PS1_MEMORY (\[rs]M),
KOSH_PS1_DOT (\[rs].), KOSH_PS1_TIME (\[rs]t), KOSH_PS1_TIME_12
(\[rs]T), KOSH_PS1_TIME_AMPM (\[rs]\[at]), KOSH_PS1_TIME_SHORT (\[rs]A),
KOSH_PS1_DATE (\[rs]d), KOSH_PS1_SHELL (\[rs]s), KOSH_PS1_VERSION
(\[rs]v), KOSH_PS1_VERSION_FULL (\[rs]V), KOSH_PS1_TTY (\[rs]l),
KOSH_PS1_HISTORY_NUMBER (\[rs]!), and KOSH_PS1_COMMAND_NUMBER (\[rs]#)
hold the current text of each escape, and assignments to them are
ignored.
.PP
The terminal title shows the user and directory while the shell waits,
and the command while a foreground command runs.
Output of PROMPT_COMMAND or PS1 can replace the idle title.
.SS History
An interactive shell records each submitted command line as one event,
even when it spans several lines, while the \f[CR]history\f[R] option is
on, which is the default.
A non\-interactive shell records no events, whatever the option says.
A line that repeats the previous event is not recorded again.
A line with a history expansion error is not recorded.
.PP
The history file is KOSH_HISTORY_FILE, \[ti]/.kosh_history by default.
An interactive shell loads it at startup into its own list and appends
each command to the file as soon as it is submitted, so other running
shells can read it.
It does not import the commands that other shells append later.
Ctrl\-R searches a snapshot of the whole file.
A non\-interactive shell writes the file only through
\f[CR]history \-w\f[R] or \f[CR]history \-a\f[R].
.PP
The file holds one event per line, with a newline in an event written as
\[rs]n and a backslash as \[rs]\[rs].
It has mode 0600, and the shell locks its directory while it writes.
A record over 65535 bytes is skipped when the file is read.
KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE is the number of events kept in the list and in the
file, 4096 by default, and 0 disables history.
When the shell exits, it rewrites the file with only the newest
KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE events.
.PP
editor.history.file_path and editor.history.max_entries set the two
variables from the settings files, and a value inherited from the
environment takes precedence.
The variables are exported only when they were inherited exported or are
exported explicitly.
The Bash history variables have no effect, as listed under Shell
variables.
\f[CR]history\f[R] and \f[CR]fc\f[R] list, edit, and run events.
.SS Koshka variables
The following variables identify Koshka and control its features.
.SS Shell\-maintained variables
The assignment and inheritance rules under Shell variables apply to
these variables.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The invocation path.
Every mood sets it without exporting it, so a line guarded by
\f[CR]test \-n \[dq]$KOSH\[dq]\f[R] runs only in Koshka.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_VERSION, KOSH_COMMIT, KOSH_BUILD_MODE, KOSH_OS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The version, build commit, build mode, and build host.
They are not exported.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_IDENTITY\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The read\-only, exported CRC\-32 of the running executable, in lowercase
hex.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_ANSI_*\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Terminal escapes in BLACK, RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN,
WHITE, their BRIGHT_ forms, BOLD, DIM, and RESET, empty when color is
disabled.
An assignment replaces the value.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_ANALYSIS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The analysis settings changed with \f[CR]set\f[R], exported as a list of
words.
The word \f[CR]mimicry\f[R] stands for \f[CR]\-I\f[R],
\f[CR]warnings=\f[R]\f[I]N\f[R] for the \f[CR]\-W\f[R] level,
\f[CR]no\-annoying\f[R] for optimizer.show_annoying_tier off, and
\f[CR]no\-diagnostics\f[R] for optimizer.analyze_before_running off.
Each \f[CR]set\f[R] rewrites it, and it is removed when empty.
A launched shell applies it, and an explicit \f[CR]\-W\f[R] replaces the
inherited level.
Command\-line options do not set it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_GIT_BRANCH, KOSH_GIT_AHEAD, KOSH_GIT_BEHIND\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The branch of the repository holding the working directory, a short hash
for a detached HEAD, or empty, and the unpushed and unpulled commit
counts against the upstream, empty when zero or unavailable.
The search stops at GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES.
An assignment replaces the branch.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_PS1_*\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The current text of each prompt escape, as described under Prompting.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSHCONF\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The current mood and interactive options in the encoding of
Configuration files.
Assignments are discarded, and the shell never exports it by itself.
.SS Configuration variables
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_HISTORY_FILE, KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The history file and its size, as described under History.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_DIRECTORY_HISTORY\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The file of visited directories that \f[CR]z\f[R] ranks,
\[ti]/.kosh_directory_history by default.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_CALC_HISTORY\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The history file of \f[CR]calc\f[R], \[ti]/.kosh_calc_history by
default.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_FLAGS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Default command\-line options, as described under Invocation.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND, KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The external selector program, fzf by default, and its options, by
default \-\-multi \-\-layout=reverse \-\-height=\[ti]40%
\-\-min\-height=3, split on blanks without expansion.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A value of 0 turns off the terminal integration marks.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]KOSH_WELCOME, KOSH_FAREWELL\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Messages printed with the shell prefix at interactive startup and exit.
An interactive shell sets them unless the environment holds them, to
Welcome :3 in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, Bash me harder!
in the Bash moods, POSIX me harder!
in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, and Goodbye :c.
An exported empty value, or a value of zero display width such as one
set by printf \-v KOSH_WELCOME \[dq]\[rs]r\[dq], disables a message, and
a startup file can set one, as in KOSH_WELCOME=$(date).
.SS Koshka builtins
The Koshka builtins assimilate, bench, koshconf, koshkit, slurp, and z
exist in every mood.
The common help and exit\-status rules are described under Builtins.
.SS \f[CR]assimilate\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]assimilate [options] [TARGET]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Copy the running executable through scp to TARGET and install it as
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] in the first usable remote PATH directory, skipping a
path that contains sbin.
Without TARGET, install it the same way into the first usable local PATH
directory and create a relative symbolic link to \f[CR]kosh\f[R] for
each bundled utility and utility alias whose name is free there or
already links to it.
.PP
On Windows the local form installs kosh.exe and makes a hard link with
the .exe suffix for each utility and link mood, and a later run with a
changed binary makes those links again.
A name that another entry in that directory holds, or that a program
elsewhere on PATH provides, stays unlinked, and each such group is
reported in one warning.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-link\-mood=\f[R]\f[I]MOOD\f[R] adds a relative bash, dash, sh,
or kosh link.
It may be repeated and takes comma\-separated values.
\f[CR]\-\-install\-directory=\f[R]\f[I]DIR\f[R] installs into DIR, even
one containing sbin.
\f[CR]\-\-ssh\-command=\f[R]\f[I]COMMAND\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-scp\-command=\f[R]\f[I]COMMAND\f[R] replace ssh and scp and
need TARGET.
\f[CR]\-x\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-trace\f[R] prints progress.
.PP
The shell asks for confirmation with the target and the full path of the
new binary, as described under Bundled utilities, and \f[CR]\-y\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] skips the question.
The copy is validated before the installed binary is replaced, and a
failed installation restores the previous file or link.
.SS \f[CR]bench\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]bench [options] command ...\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run each command through \f[CR]kosh \-c\f[R] and report the wall time,
peak resident set, and available performance counters as a mean,
standard deviation, and range.
Sampling stops after a nonzero status, and bench returns 1, unless
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-exit\-code\f[R] is given.
\f[CR]\-\-show\-output\f[R], \f[CR]\-N\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-no\-shell\f[R]),
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-runs\f[R]), and \f[CR]\-t\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-duration\f[R]) control output, direct execution, the sample
count, and the time budget in milliseconds, 3000 by default.
.PP
\f[CR]\-w\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-warmup\f[R]) sets the number of unmeasured
runs before sampling.
Without a value it runs one to three until two runs agree within 10%,
and without the option one unmeasured run precedes sampling.
The run and warmup counts range from 1 to 100000.
A note follows the report when a sample is an outlier.
.PP
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-profile\f[R]) is
\f[CR]\-\-runs 1 \-\-extended\f[R] and cannot be combined with
\f[CR]\-n\f[R].
A missing command, a count out of range, and other usage errors return
1, and an interrupt returns 130.
.PP
\f[CR]\-E\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-extended\f[R]) also reports CPU time and
usage, waiting time (wall time minus user and system time, or the
average active task count times wall time minus CPU time for a parallel
command), context switches, page faults, block operations, and read and
write calls and bytes.
Each platform adds what it can measure.
.PP
Linux adds scheduler and hardware counters and, from one extra untimed
traced run, system calls, threads, processes, execs, and task counts.
macOS adds processor counters, run\-queue wait, wakeups, energy, and
storage figures.
Windows adds whole process\-tree counters through a job object.
Traced rows are marked (traced), a row marked * counts only the measured
process, and a row the platform cannot report is omitted.
.SS \f[CR]koshconf\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]koshconf create PRESET [\-\-force]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]koshconf set OPTION VALUE [\-\-persist]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]koshconf get OPTION\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]koshconf list [\-a|\-\-all]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]koshconf load ENCODED\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]koshconf save [\-\-persist]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Read and change the Koshka settings.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]create\f[R] writes the default file of the \f[CR]kosh\f[R],
\f[CR]bash\f[R], or \f[CR]sh\f[R] preset to the user settings file, with
every setting except the mood commented out, and refuses to replace an
existing file without \f[CR]\-\-force\f[R].
It also writes the default user rc script when that is missing.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]set\f[R] changes the option for the session, and with
\f[CR]\-\-persist\f[R] also rewrites its line in the user settings file,
saving the previous file as \f[I]kosh.conf.bak\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]get\f[R] prints the value.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]list\f[R] prints every option as \f[I]name\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R] in
alphabetical order, and \f[CR]\-a\f[R] adds a line for each alias and
for the \f[CR]set\f[R] options hashall and nolog, which only
\f[CR]set\f[R] accepts.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]load\f[R] applies a \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] value to the session.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]save\f[R] prints the current mood and interactive options as a
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] value, so a kosh started with
\f[CR]KOSHCONF=$(koshconf save)\f[R] adopts them.
With \f[CR]\-\-persist\f[R], it writes the user settings file instead,
holding each setting that differs from the system file or, where the
system file does not set it, from the default of the mood.
.PP
A boolean is on or off, also accepted as true, false, 1, or 0.
An unknown option or value, or a file that cannot be read or written,
returns 1.
Invalid command syntax returns 2.
An interactive shell reports the file it wrote on standard error.
A restricted shell refuses create, set, load, and save \-\-persist.
.SS \f[CR]koshkit\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]koshkit [\-\-color when] [utility [argument ...]]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run a bundled utility, as described under Bundled utilities.
.SS \f[CR]slurp\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]slurp [\-e|\-v|\-V] [+LINE] FILE ...\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Edit each FILE in the line editor, one after another.
A missing FILE starts empty, and Ctrl\-C ends the whole run.
\f[CR]+\f[R]\f[I]LINE\f[R] opens the next FILE at that line.
.PP
\f[CR]\-e\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-emacs\f[R] selects the emacs key set,
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-vi\f[R] the vi key set in insert mode, and
\f[CR]\-V\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-vi\-normal\f[R] the vi key set in normal mode
on the first line.
The bundled utilities vi and emacs run slurp \-V and slurp \-e.
.PP
The session runs on the alternate screen.
A shell script, recognized by its shebang or extension, keeps syntax
highlighting, and the format commands change only such a file.
.PP
The top row repeats the prompt and the command, the file fills the rows
below, and the last two rows show the file kind, line endings, size, and
caret position, then the key set and the last save result or the keys of
the key menu.
A hint, a message, or a command line that needs more rows takes up to
eight while it shows.
.PP
Enter starts a new line, and Up and Down do not recall history.
Tab inserts a tab at the start of a line or in a file that is not a
shell script, and completes otherwise.
Tabs and trailing blanks are drawn shaded.
.PP
The mouse moves the caret, selects by dragging, and scrolls with the
wheel.
With the emacs key set, Shift with a movement key also selects.
.PP
Ctrl\-X s, Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-S, \f[CR]:w\f[R], and \f[CR]:write\f[R] save
and keep the session open.
\f[CR]:wq\f[R], \f[CR]:wq!\f[R], \f[CR]:wqa\f[R], \f[CR]:wqa!\f[R], and
\f[CR]:x\f[R] save and leave when the save succeeds.
.PP
\f[CR]:q\f[R], Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C, and Ctrl\-D on an empty buffer leave
without saving, and Ctrl\-C leaves with status 130.
While the buffer has unsaved changes, \f[CR]:q\f[R] refuses, the other
keys and M\-x kill\-kosh must be pressed twice, and \f[CR]:q!\f[R]
leaves at once.
.PP
\f[CR]:\f[R]N and \f[CR]:$\f[R] move to line N and the last line.
Ctrl\-S and Ctrl\-R, or \f[CR]/\f[R] and \f[CR]?\f[R], find text as it
is typed.
\f[CR]help\f[R] in M\-x and \f[CR]:help\f[R] list the commands.
.PP
A save writes a complete copy beside the file and renames it over the
file, keeping its mode and following a symbolic link.
A file that another user owns, a file with several links, or a file in a
directory that neither the user nor root owns, or that others can write
without the sticky bit, is rewritten in place, keeping its owner, group,
and links, after a private backup copy is made in the home or temporary
directory.
.PP
The copy is removed after a successful write and reported after a failed
one, and without a copy nothing is written.
A save is refused when another file has replaced the loaded one.
A failed save is reported and the edits stay open.
CRLF line endings and the final newline are kept.
.PP
An empty operand, a directory, a special or unreadable file, a file with
a NUL byte or invalid UTF\-8, and a file over 16 MiB are refused with
status 1, and the next FILE still opens.
A restricted shell, or standard input or output that is not a terminal,
is refused with status 1.
A missing FILE, a \f[CR]+\f[R]\f[I]LINE\f[R] without a FILE, or more
than one mode option returns 2.
.SS \f[CR]z\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]z [query ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Change to a directory that interactive shells have visited.
A single operand that is an existing directory is used as it is.
Otherwise the operands form one case\-insensitive substring query,
ranked by visit count and recency.
With no operand, z changes to the home directory.
No matching directory returns 1.
.SS Bundled utilities
The \f[CR]koshkit\f[R] builtin runs a bundled utility, and an unknown
utility is an error.
With no utility it groups the catalog under POSIX and Koshka headings.
\f[CR]koshkit \-\-list\f[R] prints the utilities one per line, and
\f[CR]koshkit \-\-list\-flags\f[R] prints each utility and alias with
its short and long flags.
.PP
The bundled set is basename, bc, cal, calc, cat, chgrp, chmod, chown,
cksum, cmp, comm, cp, csplit, cut, date, df, diff, dirname, du, env,
evil, evildisk, evilfiles, evilfs, evilio, eviliso, evillogs, evilnet,
evilps, evilss, expand, expr, file, find, flock, fold, fuser, getconf,
goodcore, goodfsw, goodnode, goodstat, grep, head, id, killall, less,
link, ln, locale, logger, logname, ls, make, man, meow, mkdir, mkfifo,
mknod, more, mv, nice, nl, nohup, nproc, od, paste, pathchk, pkill, pr,
printenv, ps, readlink, realpath, renice, retry, rm, rmdir, sed, seq,
sleep, slurp, sort, split, stat, strings, stty, sync, tabs, tail, tee,
timeout, touch, tput, tr, tsort, tty, uname, unexpand, uniq, unlink,
watch, wc, which, who, whoami, xargs, and yes, with the aliases egrep
and fgrep for grep and vi and emacs for slurp.
.PP
Every utility supports \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-version\f[R]
when invoked under its own name.
Options may come before and after operands, and \f[CR]\-\-\f[R] ends
them.
calc, env, expr, flock, nice, nohup, retry, timeout, watch, and xargs
end their options at the first operand, so the command after it keeps
its own options.
.PP
A utility with a styled report, such as cal, cat, du, ls, watch, meow,
and the evil and good utilities, uses color only when its output is a
terminal and NO_COLOR is empty or unset.
\f[CR]koshkit \-\-color\f[R] \f[I]when\f[R] selects always, never, or
auto for the utility that follows, and diff and grep also take
\f[CR]\-\-color\f[R].
tabs and tput write escape sequences wherever their output goes.
.PP
A utility returns 0 on success, 1 when an operand fails, and 2 for a
usage error, such as an unknown flag or a missing operand.
A failure on one file is reported and the utility goes on with the next.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood a usage error ends the script, as described
under Shell errors.
An unknown utility returns 1.
A diagnostic names the utility, as in Koshkit \[ga]seq\[ga]: for the
builtin and seq: for a utility started under its own name.
.PP
A confirmation question, such as one from \f[CR]rm \-i\f[R],
\f[CR]cp \-i\f[R], \f[CR]mv \-i\f[R], or \f[CR]assimilate\f[R], is
written to standard error after the shell prefix and followed by [y/N]
or [Y/n], the default in capitals.
The answer is read from standard input up to a newline.
An empty line takes the default, an answer beginning with y or Y
confirms, and any other answer or end of input declines.
.PP
When direct koshkit lookup is enabled, a PATH program takes precedence
over a bundled utility.
Started under a utility name, the executable runs that utility.
The name is matched with its extension removed and with its case kept.
Started as \f[CR]koshkit\f[R], the link that \f[CR]make install\f[R]
places beside \f[CR]kosh\f[R], it runs the utility in its first operand,
as in \f[CR]koshkit ls \-l\f[R], and otherwise acts as the koshkit
builtin.
Either form skips shell startup, settings, and KOSH_FLAGS.
.SS \f[CR]calc\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]calc\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate arithmetic operands with arbitrary\-precision integers and
finite decimals.
\f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-interactive\f[R] and \f[CR]\-p\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-pipe\f[R] read expressions interactively or from a pipe.
The functions are abs, atan, ceil, cmp, cos, exp, fact, fib, floor,
frac, gcd, int, iseven, isint, isodd, length, lcm, ln, max, min, scale,
sgn, sin, and sqrt.
Ctrl\-R searches the calc history.
.SS \f[CR]evil\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]evil and the evil utilities\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[CR]evil\f[R] prints system identity, uptime, processor, memory, swap,
file system, process, and network information; \f[CR]\-a\f[R] adds
details, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] selects identity fields, \f[CR]\-u\f[R] lists
local users.
.PP
\f[CR]evildisk\f[R] prints capacity, file system identity, and disk
error and retry totals, and with \f[CR]\-a\f[R] labels, UUIDs, error
counters, and SMART data where available.
.PP
\f[CR]evilfiles\f[R] lists open files, filtered by \f[CR]\-p\f[R]
(process), \f[CR]\-u\f[R] (owner), \f[CR]\-c\f[R] (command prefix),
\f[CR]\-i\f[R] (sockets), and \f[CR]\-t\f[R] (terse pids), with
\f[CR]\-w\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-wide\f[R] for unshortened columns.
.PP
\f[CR]evilfs\f[R] prints mounted file systems.
.PP
\f[CR]evillogs\f[R] prints core dump settings, dump reports, and log
directories, or only one with \f[CR]\-\-cores\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-logs\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]eviliso\f[R] prints namespaces (\f[CR]\-n\f[R]), cgroups
(\f[CR]\-c\f[R]), login sessions (\f[CR]\-s\f[R]), remote sockets
(\f[CR]\-r\f[R]), container runtime data (\f[CR]\-k\f[R]),
\f[CR]\-\-container\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-kubernetes\f[R] sections, all of
them without a selector, with \f[CR]\-a\f[R] for detailed rows.
The Kubernetes section reads KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST, the
service\-account namespace file, and kubepods cgroups.
.PP
\f[CR]evilss\f[R] prints TCP, UDP, and Unix sockets.
\f[CR]\-l\f[R] (listening), \f[CR]\-a\f[R] (all), \f[CR]\-t\f[R] (TCP),
\f[CR]\-u\f[R] (UDP), \f[CR]\-x\f[R] (Unix), \f[CR]\-4\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-6\f[R] select sockets, \f[CR]\-p\f[R] adds the owning process,
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] keeps addresses numeric, and \f[CR]\-H\f[R] omits the
header.
.PP
\f[CR]evilps\f[R] and \f[CR]evildisk\f[R] take optional process ids and
files, and \f[CR]evilfiles\f[R] optional paths, to limit their reports.
.PP
\f[CR]evilio\f[R] prints memory, swap, and cumulative disk counters;
\f[CR]\-a\f[R] adds processor, paging, scheduler, pressure, disk
activity, and swap rates from a one\-second sample, \f[CR]\-h\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-human\-readable\f[R] (the default) uses binary units, and
\f[CR]\-\-ps\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-p\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\f[R]\f[I]NUMBER\f[R] print per\-process counters for every
process, a limited table, one process, or NUMBER rows.
.PP
\f[CR]evilnet\f[R] prints IPv4 and IPv6 addresses; \f[CR]\-a\f[R] adds
traffic, errors, drops, and TCP counters, \f[CR]\-f\f[R] TCP failure and
loss counters only, and \f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-traffic\f[R] the
traffic table only.
.PP
\f[CR]evilps\f[R] prints processes as a tree; \f[CR]\-p\f[R],
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-A\f[R], \f[CR]\-U\f[R],
\f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-M\f[R], and \f[CR]\-w\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-wide\f[R]
show pids, sort numerically, show every field, command lines, owners,
CPU, memory, and full\-length lines, and \f[CR]\-\f[R]\f[I]NUMBER\f[R]
limits the rows.
.PP
For evilio, evilnet, evilps, and evilss, \f[CR]\-l\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-live\f[R][=\f[I]N\f[R]] (only \f[CR]\-\-live\f[R] for evilss)
refreshes every N seconds, 0.5 by default, on the alternate screen until
q (status 0) or an interrupt (status 130); a tall frame scrolls with j,
k, the arrow and page keys, g, G, and the mouse wheel.
In evilio, evilnet, and evilps, \f[CR]\-\-cumulative\f[R][=\f[I]M\f[R]]
(\f[CR]\-C\f[R] in evilps) reports the change over M seconds, 1 by
default, or a rolling M\-second window with \f[CR]\-\-live\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-sort\f[R] orders rows by a metric or its shortest unambiguous
prefix: pid, read, write, read\-ops, and write\-ops for processes; also
busy, read\-latency, write\-latency, average\-queue, queue, errors, and
retries for disks; name, bytes, packets, errors, or drops for traffic;
and name, pid, cpu, or memory in evilps, where s cycles the key and /
searches in live view.
.SS \f[CR]goodcore\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]goodcore\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Capture a running process with \f[CR]\-p\f[R], or package an existing
core with its executable with \f[CR]\-b\f[R].
The archive holds the core in dump/core, the executable and its
libraries under root/, and debug\-gdb.sh and debug\-lldb.sh, which open
the core in gdb or lldb with root/ as the system root and pass their
arguments to the debugger.
.PP
The archive is goodcore\-\f[I]core\f[R].tar.zst, or .tar.gz when zstd is
missing, or the path of \f[CR]\-o\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-output\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\-no\-compress\f[R] writes a plain tar.
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R] prints only errors.
The host tar and zstd create the archive, and gdb finds the libraries.
.SS \f[CR]goodfsw\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]goodfsw\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Report path changes by scanning metadata, at most once per latency
period.
On Linux a scan follows an inotify event and a quiet tree is rescanned
every 30 periods; elsewhere, or when a watch fails, a scan runs every
period.
Each record holds the scan time, a numeric event mask, and the path.
.PP
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-human\-readable\f[R] uses timestamps and event
labels, and \f[CR]\-m\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-machine\-readable\f[R] the numeric
form; \f[CR]\-\-timezone\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-precision\f[R] format human
timestamps.
\f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-event\-flags\f[R], \f[CR]\-x\f[R],
\f[CR]\-1\f[R], \f[CR]\-l\f[R], and \f[CR]\-e\f[R] select recursion,
appended labels, one file system, one batch, the latency, and an
excluded substring.
.SS \f[CR]goodnode\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]goodnode\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print inode identity, allocation, file system capabilities, verification
state, and a CRC32C checksum for each path.
\f[CR]\-i\f[R] locates an inode beneath the \f[CR]\-r\f[R] path, and
\f[CR]\-\-verify\f[R] runs the native file system verification where
supported.
.SS \f[CR]goodstat\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]goodstat\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print file metadata as a labeled report.
\f[CR]\-L\f[R] follows links, \f[CR]\-c\f[R] adds a CRC32C checksum, and
\f[CR]\-f\f[R] a file system capacity report.
.SS \f[CR]meow\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]meow\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Show a screensaver of pixel\-art cats.
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-fps\f[R] sets frames per second (1 to 240,
default 60), \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-speed\f[R] the speed (0.1 to 10),
and \f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-cats\f[R] the number of cats (1 to 8).
Space pauses, + and \- change speed, and any other key exits.
.SS \f[CR]retry\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]retry\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Repeat a command until it succeeds or the attempts run out.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-b\f[R], \f[CR]\-m\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-q\f[R] set the attempt count, initial delay, backoff factor,
maximum delay, and quiet mode.
.SS \f[CR]slurp, vi, emacs\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]slurp, vi, emacs\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Edit text files as the \f[CR]slurp\f[R] builtin does, with the same
options and +\f[I]LINE\f[R] operand.
As \f[CR]vi\f[R] it runs with \f[CR]\-V\f[R], and as \f[CR]emacs\f[R]
with \f[CR]\-e\f[R].
.SS Windows
On Windows the shell runs the same grammar.
A path of /dev/null opens the null device, and /tmp or a path under it
maps into the directory in TEMP.
The options compatibility.dev_null and compatibility.slash_tmp, both on,
govern the two mappings.
Variable names are not case\-sensitive, PATH uses semicolons, and the
user settings directory falls back to USERPROFILE when HOME is unset.
.PP
The Bash startup files keep their POSIX paths.
A subshell runs in a new process.
Process substitution, \f[CR]echo\f[R], \f[CR]pwd \-W\f[R], and the local
form of \f[CR]assimilate\f[R] behave as their entries describe.
.SS Runtime files
\f[B]\f[CB]/etc/kosh.conf, %ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]kosh.conf\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The system settings file on POSIX and on Windows, described in
Configuration files.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf, \[ti]/.config/kosh/kosh.conf\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The user settings file.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh, \[ti]/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The rc script of an interactive or login \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood shell.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[ti]/.kosh_history\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The default history file, as described under History.
The shell writes temporary files beside it while it rewrites it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[ti]/.kosh_directory_history\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The default directory history of \f[CR]z\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[ti]/.kosh_calc_history\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The default history of \f[CR]calc\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]/etc/profile, \[ti]/.profile, \[ti]/.bash_profile, \[ti]/.bash_login, \[ti]/.bashrc, /etc/bash.bashrc\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The startup files of the \f[CR]sh\f[R] and Bash moods, as described
under Bash and POSIX startup files.
.SH BASH AND POSIX REFERENCE
.SS Invocation
The shell selects the input source in the following order.
The \f[CR]\-s\f[R] option selects standard input, one or more
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] options select their command strings, a script operand
selects that file, and \f[CR]\-i\f[R] or a terminal on standard input
selects the interactive editor.
Otherwise the shell takes commands from standard input.
.PP
When the options select several sources, the shell prints a warning and
uses the first of them in that order, except that \f[CR]\-s\f[R] with
\f[CR]\-i\f[R] starts the interactive editor when standard input is a
terminal.
The interactive editor needs a terminal, so \f[CR]\-i\f[R] without one
fails after the startup files run.
.PP
The invocation basename selects an initial mood.
The basename \f[CR]sh\f[R] or \f[CR]dash\f[R] selects the \f[CR]sh\f[R]
mood, and \f[CR]bash\f[R] or \f[CR]rbash\f[R] selects the
\f[CR]bash\f[R] mood.
\f[CR]rbash\f[R] also requests restricted mode.
A dash at the start of argument zero, or \f[CR]\-l\f[R], selects login
operation.
.PP
\f[CR]KOSH_FLAGS\f[R] supplies options before the command line, and a
command\-line option overrides the same option from it.
\f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-as\-language\-server\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-rcfile\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-init\-file\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-\f[R], and
every operand are ignored there.
A secure execution ignores and removes \f[CR]KOSH_FLAGS\f[R].
.PP
When argument zero begins with a dash and the options cannot be parsed,
the shell reports the error and enters rescue mode, which skips the
startup and settings files.
When only KOSH_FLAGS fails to parse, the shell drops it and keeps the
command\-line options and input source.
When the command line fails to parse, the shell keeps only
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] and takes commands from standard input, interactively on
a terminal.
Any other invalid invocation, including an unknown value of
\f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-tab\-selector\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-init\-moods\f[R], \f[CR]\-o\f[R], or \f[CR]\-O\f[R], exits
with status 2.
.SS Operands
\f[B]\f[CB]script\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
When neither \f[CR]\-s\f[R] nor \f[CR]\-c\f[R] selects another source,
the first operand is the script file.
A lone \f[CR]\-\f[R] selects standard input.
A directory fails with status 126, and a file that cannot be opened
fails with status 127.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]argument\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
For a script, the script path becomes $0 and later arguments become
positional parameters.
For \f[CR]\-c\f[R], the first operand after COMMAND becomes $0.
For \f[CR]\-s\f[R] and interactive operation, $0 stays the invocation
name and every operand becomes a positional parameter.
.SS Bash and POSIX startup files
A login shell in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood sources /etc/profile and
\[ti]/.profile.
An interactive \f[CR]sh\f[R] shell sources the file in ENV, without a
PATH search, unless privileged mode is on.
.PP
A login shell in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] or \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] mood
sources /etc/profile, then the first readable file of
\[ti]/.bash_profile, \[ti]/.bash_login, and \[ti]/.profile.
An interactive non\-login shell in those moods sources the first
readable file of /etc/bash/bashrc and /etc/bash.bashrc, then the custom
rc file or \[ti]/.bashrc.
.PP
When it has sourced one of them, tab completion is on, no default
completion spec exists, and BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO is unset, the shell
then sources /usr/share/bash\-completion/bash_completion.
A non\-interactive shell in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood, including a login
shell after its profiles and a \f[CR]kosh\f[R] session started with
\f[CR]\-L bash\f[R], sources BASH_ENV once, without a PATH search.
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] does not.
A custom rc file applies only to an interactive non\-login shell.
On Windows these files keep their POSIX paths.
.PP
The rules for skipping startup files are described under Startup files.
.SS Terminology
\f[B]\f[CB]blank\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A space or a tab.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A sequence of characters that the shell treats as one unit, also called
a token.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]identifier\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A word of letters, digits, and underscores that does not begin with a
digit.
Variables and, in most contexts, functions are identifiers.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]metacharacter\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A character that separates words when it is unquoted.
The metacharacters are a blank, a newline, and | & ; ( ) < >.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]control operator\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A token that performs a control function.
The control operators are a newline and ||, &&, &, ;, ;;, ;&, ;;&, |,
|&, (, and ).
.SS Reserved words
A reserved word has a special meaning when it is unquoted and appears as
the first word of a command, as the third word of a \f[CR]case\f[R] or
\f[CR]for\f[R] command, or after another reserved word that starts a
command list.
The reserved words are
.PP
!
case coproc do done elif else esac fi for function if in select then
time until while { } [[ ]]
.PP
\f[CR]select\f[R] is reserved in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] and
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] moods only.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood does not reserve \f[CR]coproc\f[R],
\f[CR]select\f[R], or \f[CR]time\f[R], and it reports [[ as a dialect
error.
A reserved word in any other position is an ordinary word, so
\f[CR]echo if then fi\f[R] prints its arguments.
.SS Shell grammar
The shell accepts the POSIX Shell Command Language and the Bash
extensions described in this manual.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood follows dash.
It reports [[ ]], the C\-style for loop, |&, and <<< as dialect errors,
and a script holding one does not run.
It does not recognize $\[aq]...\[aq], $\[dq]...\[dq], brace expansion,
&>, &>>, >&\f[I]file\f[R], (( )), $[ ], ${ } function substitution, the
${\f[I]v\f[R]\[at]\f[I]op\f[R]} transformations, or quotes inside $((
)).
.PP
It still accepts process substitution, the \f[CR]function\f[R] keyword,
and subscript assignment, and it assigns an empty string for a compound
assignment such as a=(x y).
.SS Simple commands
A simple command is a sequence of optional variable assignments followed
by words and redirections, ended by a control operator.
The first word is the command, and the remaining words are its
arguments.
The status of a simple command is its exit status, or 128 plus the
signal number when a signal ends it.
.PP
An assignment before the command word applies to the environment of that
command only.
The values of the assignments in one prefix are expanded before any of
them is made, so in \f[CR]a=7 b=$a cmd\f[R] the variable b receives the
previous value of a.
After a special builtin, the assignments remain in the shell in the
\f[CR]kosh\f[R], \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods, and
they are removed in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood.
After a function call they are removed in every mood.
A command with assignments and no command word makes the assignments in
the shell.
.SS Pipelines
A pipeline is a sequence of one or more commands separated by | or |&,
in the following form.
.PP
[\f[CR]time\f[R] [\f[CR]\-p\f[R]] [\f[CR]\-R\f[R]]] [\f[CR]!\f[R]]
\f[I]command\f[R] [ { | | |& } \f[I]command\f[R] ...
]
.PP
The standard output of each command is connected to the standard input
of the next.
The form |& also connects standard error, as 2>&1 | does.
Each command runs in its own process, with these exceptions.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods, a builtin in the last
stage runs in the shell, so \f[CR]echo 5 | read x\f[R] sets x.
.PP
In the \f[CR]bash\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] moods, the last stage
runs in the shell only while the \f[CR]lastpipe\f[R] option is on and
job control is off.
A function or a compound command in the last stage runs in its own
process in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods.
.PP
The status of a pipeline is the status of its last command.
While the \f[CR]pipefail\f[R] option is on, it is the status of the
rightmost command that failed, or 0.
A leading !
negates the status.
PIPESTATUS holds the status of each stage of the last foreground
pipeline.
A stage that expands to no command, such as \f[CR]p=1 | cat\f[R], is an
error in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, and the other moods apply its
redirections and give it status 0.
.PP
The reserved word \f[CR]time\f[R] reports the elapsed, user, and system
time of the pipeline after it finishes.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood the report also has a processor usage row
and, when it is nonzero and the platform reports it, the peak resident
set of the pipeline and its descendants.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] selects the POSIX format, which has no processor row, and
\f[CR]\-R\f[R] adds the resident set row in every format.
.PP
TIMEFORMAT selects the format, as described under Shell variables.
\f[CR]time\f[R] with no pipeline prints nothing.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, \f[CR]time\f[R] is an ordinary command found
through PATH.
.SS Lists
A list is a sequence of pipelines separated by ;, &, &&, or ||, and
optionally ended by ;, &, or a newline.
The operators && and || have equal precedence and group from the left.
They bind more tightly than ; and &, which also have equal precedence.
.PP
\f[I]command1\f[R] \f[CR]&&\f[R] \f[I]command2\f[R] runs command2 only
when command1 returns 0, and \f[I]command1\f[R] \f[CR]||\f[R]
\f[I]command2\f[R] runs command2 only when command1 returns nonzero.
The status of an and\-or list is the status of the last command that
ran.
.PP
A list ended by ; or a newline runs in the foreground, and the shell
waits for it.
An ampersand runs the whole and\-or list before it asynchronously, so
\f[CR]a && b || c &\f[R] is one background job, and its variable and
directory changes stay in the job.
The status of an asynchronous list is 0, and $!
holds its process id.
.PP
In the Bash and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods, an asynchronous simple command
starts its process before it expands its words, assignments, and
redirections, so an expansion error fails the job.
The status is 127 for an unset parameter or ${name?word} and 1 for a
failed redirection in the Bash moods, and 2 in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood expands the words before it starts the job.
.SS Compound commands
A compound command can be followed by redirections, which apply to the
whole command.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](list)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run list in a subshell.
Changes to variables, the working directory, options, and traps do not
reach the shell.
The status is the status of list.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]{ list; }\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run list in the shell.
The braces are reserved words, so they must be separate words, and list
must end with a semicolon or a newline.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]((expression))\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate expression as described under Arithmetic evaluation.
The status is 0 when the value is nonzero, and 1 when the value is zero
or the expression is empty.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood treats (( as two subshell openings.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][[ expression ]]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate a conditional expression, as described under Conditional
expressions.
The status is 0 or 1, and 2 when the expression cannot be evaluated.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]for name [ [in [word ...]] ; ] do list ; done\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Expand the words and run list once for each resulting field, with name
set to the field.
Without \f[CR]in\f[R], the loop iterates over the positional parameters.
The status is the status of the last list that ran, or 0 when the loop
did not iterate.
The body must be a \f[CR]do\f[R] ...
\f[CR]done\f[R] list, and a brace group is rejected.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]for (( expr1 ; expr2 ; expr3 )) ; do list ; done\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate expr1, then run list and evaluate expr3 as long as expr2 is
nonzero.
An empty expression counts as 1.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood reports this form as a dialect error.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]select name [in word ...] ; do list ; done\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the expanded words as a numbered menu on standard error, print
PS3, and read a line.
A number of a word sets name to that word, any other line sets name to
empty, and REPLY holds the line.
The body runs after each nonempty line until a \f[CR]break\f[R] or the
end of input, and an empty line prints the menu again.
Only the \f[CR]bash\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] moods accept select.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]case word in [ [(] pattern [ | pattern ] ... ) list terminator ] ... esac\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Expand word and run the list of the first pattern that matches it, as
described under Pattern matching.
The terminator ;; ends the command, ;& also runs the next list without
testing its pattern, and ;;& tests the next patterns.
The status is the status of the last list that ran, or 0 when no pattern
matched.
Every mood accepts the three terminators.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]if list; then list; [ elif list; then list; ] ... [ else list; ] fi\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run the then list of the first condition list that returns 0, or the
else list.
The status is the status of the last list that ran, or 0 when no branch
ran.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]while list; do list; done\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]until list; do list; done\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run the body as long as the condition list returns 0, or, for until,
nonzero.
The status is the status of the last body that ran, or 0.
.SS Coprocesses
.IP
.EX
coproc [NAME] command [redirections]
.EE
.PP
Every mood except \f[CR]sh\f[R] accepts a coprocess.
The command runs asynchronously, connected to the shell by two pipes.
The shell receives the output of the coprocess from NAME[0] and writes
its input to NAME[1], and NAME_PID holds the process id.
NAME defaults to COPROC, and a word before the command is NAME only when
a compound command follows it.
.PP
The launch returns status 0, and $!
holds the process id.
The descriptors are above 9, close across an exec, and are not passed to
subshells.
When the coprocess finishes, the shell closes them and unsets NAME and
NAME_PID.
.SS Function definitions
.IP
.EX
fname () command [redirections]
 function fname [()] command [redirections]
.EE
.PP
A function definition stores command as the body of fname and returns 0.
The body can be any command, including a simple command, as in
\f[CR]f() echo hi\f[R].
The redirections apply to every call.
A later definition replaces the body.
A function made read\-only with \f[CR]readonly \-f\f[R] cannot be
redefined.
.PP
A function name can hold any characters that form one unquoted word,
such as f.g or f\-g, and a quoted word also works as a name.
A reserved word after \f[CR]function\f[R] is an error, and a name built
by an expansion defines nothing.
Calling a function is described under Functions.
.SS Comments
An unquoted # at the start of a word begins a comment that lasts to the
end of the line.
A # inside a word, as in a#b, is an ordinary character.
An interactive shell recognizes comments while the
\f[CR]interactive_comments\f[R] option is on.
The option is on by default.
.SS Quoting
Quoting removes the special meaning of characters and keeps a word from
being split or expanded.
.PP
An unquoted backslash preserves the next character, except that a
backslash before a newline joins the two lines.
A backslash at the very end of the input is an error in the
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood and a literal backslash in the other moods.
On Windows an unquoted backslash is also an escape, so a native path
needs a quoted or doubled backslash or a forward slash.
.PP
Single quotes preserve every enclosed character.
A single quote cannot appear between single quotes, even after a
backslash.
.PP
Double quotes preserve every enclosed character except $, \[ga], and
\[rs].
Parameter expansion, command substitution, and arithmetic expansion take
place inside them.
A backslash inside double quotes keeps its meaning only before $, \[ga],
\[dq], \[rs], or a newline.
Inside double quotes, \[dq]$\[at]\[dq] and \[dq]${a[\[at]]}\[dq] still
produce one word per element.
.PP
A word of the form $\[aq]\f[I]string\f[R]\[aq] expands backslash escapes
and is otherwise single\-quoted.
The escapes are \[rs]a, \[rs]b, \[rs]e and \[rs]E (escape), \[rs]f,
\[rs]n, \[rs]r, \[rs]t, \[rs]v, \[rs]\[rs], \[rs]\[aq], \[rs]\[dq],
\[rs]?, \[rs]x\f[I]H\f[R] and \[rs]x\f[I]HH\f[R] (hexadecimal),
\[rs]\f[I]NNN\f[R] (one to three octal digits), \[rs]u\f[I]HHHH\f[R] and
\[rs]U\f[I]HHHHHHHH\f[R] (a Unicode character in UTF\-8), and
\[rs]c\f[I]X\f[R] (a control character).
Any other escape stays as written, with its backslash.
A NUL byte ends the string.
.PP
A word of the form $\[dq]\f[I]string\f[R]\[dq] marks the string for
translation, and it acts as a double\-quoted string.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood recognizes neither form and keeps the $ as an
ordinary character.
.SS Parameters
A parameter holds a value.
A variable is a parameter denoted by an identifier, a positional
parameter is denoted by a number, and a special parameter by one of the
characters listed below.
A variable is set when it has been assigned a value, which may be empty,
and it stays set until it is unset.
.PP
An assignment has the form \f[I]name\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R].
The value undergoes tilde expansion, parameter expansion, command
substitution, arithmetic expansion, and quote removal, but no field
splitting or pathname expansion.
A tilde expands after the = and after each colon of the value.
The form \f[I]name\f[R]+=\f[I]value\f[R] appends to a string, adds to an
integer, and appends elements to an array.
.PP
\f[CR]declare\f[R] and \f[CR]local\f[R] give variables attributes, such
as integer, read\-only, exported, or a case conversion, as described
under Builtins.
.SS Positional parameters
The positional parameters are the arguments of the shell, a function, or
a sourced file, numbered from 1.
A parameter above 9 needs braces, so $10 is $1 followed by a 0 and ${10}
is the tenth parameter.
\f[CR]set \-\-\f[R] replaces them, and \f[CR]shift\f[R] discards the
first ones.
They cannot be assigned directly, and ${\[at]:=x} is an error.
.PP
An unquoted $\[at] or $* is subject to field splitting.
\[dq]$\[at]\[dq] keeps one field per parameter, and \[dq]$*\[dq] joins
the parameters with the first byte of IFS.
A $\[at] that becomes one string, as in an assignment, joins with the
first byte of IFS in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood and with a space in the Bash
moods.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, as in dash, $\[at] and $* are always set, so
\[dq]${\[at]\-d}\[dq] and \[dq]${\[at]+p}\[dq] never test for missing
parameters.
${\[at]:\f[I]offset\f[R]:\f[I]length\f[R]} selects parameters, and an
offset of 0 includes $0.
.SS Special parameters
\f[B]\f[CB]*\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The positional parameters, joined as described under Special parameters.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[at]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The positional parameters, one field each inside double quotes.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]#\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The number of positional parameters.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]?\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The status of the last command, pipeline, or command substitution.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The letters of the options that are on, as described under the
\f[CR]set\f[R] builtin.
The letters h and B are on by default, c appears for a command string,
and u appears in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]$\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The process id of the shell.
A subshell and a command substitution keep the value of the shell that
started them.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]!\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The process id of the last asynchronous command, coprocess, or process
substitution.
It is empty before the first one.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]0\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The name of the shell or the script, as described under Operands.
Inside a function it stays the shell or script name.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]_\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The last argument of the previous simple command.
It is empty at startup.
An assignment with no command and a [[ ]] command do not change it.
.SS Shell variables
The shell sets the following variables.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood sets only PIPESTATUS, LINENO, OPTIND, PPID,
SHLVL, PWD, OLDPWD, IFS, PS2, and PS4 from this list, along with the
shell\-maintained variables listed under Koshka variables.
.PP
An assignment to a variable that the shell maintains, such as BASHPID or
HISTCMD, is discarded unless the entry states otherwise.
The shell ignores a value of such a variable inherited from the
environment, and children receive the inherited value unchanged.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH, BASH_VERSION, BASH_VERSINFO\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A Bash identity, set when the mood or an initialized mood is
\f[CR]bash\f[R] or \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R].
BASH holds the executable, BASH_VERSION is 5.3.0(1)\-kosh, and
BASH_VERSINFO holds the version fields, with the build host in element
5.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_ALIASES\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The aliases.
Assigning an element defines an alias, unsetting an element does
nothing, and unsetting the array disconnects it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_ARGC, BASH_ARGV\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The argument count of each recorded frame, and the arguments in reverse
order, innermost first.
The \f[CR]extdebug\f[R] option records function and source calls in
them.
Without it BASH_ARGV is empty and BASH_ARGC holds one 0.
Assignments do nothing, and unset and local fail.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_ARGV0\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Argument zero of the shell.
An assignment does not change $0.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_COMMAND\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The command that is running or about to run.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_EXECUTION_STRING\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The command string of \f[CR]\-c\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_LINENO, BASH_SOURCE, FUNCNAME\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The call stack.
FUNCNAME[0] is the running function, BASH_SOURCE[i] is the file that
defines FUNCNAME[i], and BASH_LINENO[i] is the line where FUNCNAME[i]
was called.
They are empty in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_MONOSECONDS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A monotonic clock in milliseconds.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_REMATCH\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The match and the groups of the last successful [[ =\[ti] ]], and empty
after a failed match.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_SUBSHELL\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The subshell depth, 0 in the shell.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASHOPTS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The read\-only, colon\-separated list of the \f[CR]shopt\f[R] options
that are on.
A value in the environment is ignored.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASHPID\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The process id of the current shell or subshell.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]COMP_CWORD, COMP_LINE, COMP_POINT, COMP_WORDS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The cursor word index, the line, the cursor byte offset, and the words,
for a completion function, as described under \f[CR]complete\f[R].
\f[CR]COMP_KEY\f[R] and \f[CR]COMP_TYPE\f[R] are exported to a
\f[CR]complete \-C\f[R] command only.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]COPROC, COPROC_PID\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The descriptors and process of a coprocess without NAME.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]DIRSTACK\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The current directory followed by the saved stack.
Assigning an existing index other than 0 changes that entry, other
assignments and element unsets do nothing, and unsetting the array
disconnects it.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]EPOCHREALTIME, EPOCHSECONDS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The wall time in seconds, with microseconds in EPOCHREALTIME.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]EUID, UID\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The read\-only effective and real user ids.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]GROUPS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The real group id.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]HISTCMD\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The history number of the current command, 0 in a non\-interactive
shell.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]HOSTNAME, HOSTTYPE, MACHTYPE, OSTYPE\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The host and the platform.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]LINENO\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The line of the running command.
Inside a command substitution that spans several lines, each command
reports its own line in the file.
Assignments are discarded, and unset keeps its meaning.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]MAPFILE\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The default array of \f[CR]mapfile\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]OLDPWD, PWD\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The previous and current logical working directories, updated by
\f[CR]cd\f[R].
They are exported only when they were inherited exported or are exported
explicitly.
A logical PWD is accepted at startup only when it is absolute and
identifies the current directory.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]OPTARG, OPTIND\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The argument of the last option and the index of the next argument for
\f[CR]getopts\f[R].
OPTIND starts at 1.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]PIPESTATUS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The status of each stage of the last foreground pipeline.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]PPID\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The process id of the parent of the shell.
It is read\-only except in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]RANDOM\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A random integer from 0 to 32767.
Assigning it seeds the sequence reproducibly, and unsetting it makes it
an ordinary variable.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]REPLY\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The line read by \f[CR]read\f[R] without a variable, and the line read
by \f[CR]select\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]SECONDS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The seconds since the shell started.
Assigning it moves its base, and unsetting it makes it an ordinary
variable.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]SHELLOPTS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The read\-only, colon\-separated list of the \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] options
that are on.
A value in the environment is ignored.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]SHLVL\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The nesting level of shells, incremented at startup.
A negative inherited value becomes 1, and an inherited value of 1000 or
more restarts at 1.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]SRANDOM\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A random 32\-bit integer from the system generator.
.PP
The following variables affect the shell.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
When set, an interactive \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood shell does not source the
bash\-completion script.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_ENV, ENV\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Startup files, as described under Bash and POSIX startup files.
Their values undergo parameter expansion and command substitution.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]BASH_XTRACEFD\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The descriptor that receives \f[CR]xtrace\f[R] output.
Standard error receives it when the variable is unset or invalid.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]CDPATH\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The colon\-separated directories that \f[CR]cd\f[R] searches for a
relative operand.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]COLUMNS, LINES\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The terminal size, set at the start of an interactive shell.
They are not updated when the terminal changes size.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]FCEDIT, EDITOR, VISUAL\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
FCEDIT, then EDITOR, selects the editor of \f[CR]fc\f[R].
VISUAL, then EDITOR, selects the editor of Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]GLOBIGNORE\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Colon\-separated patterns of pathname expansion results to remove, as
described under Pathname expansion.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]HOME\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The home directory, used by \f[CR]cd\f[R] and tilde expansion.
On Windows the shell uses USERPROFILE when HOME is unset.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]IFS\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The field separators and the joiner of \[dq]$*\[dq], by default space,
tab, and newline.
A value in the environment is ignored.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]IGNOREEOF\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The number of consecutive end\-of\-file inputs that an interactive shell
needs before it exits while \f[CR]ignoreeof\f[R] is on.
Enabling the option sets an unset IGNOREEOF to 10, assigning IGNOREEOF
enables the option, and unsetting it disables the option.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]LANG, LC_ALL, LC_COLLATE, LC_CTYPE, LC_NUMERIC\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The locale, as described under Locale.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]MANPATH\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The manual page directories that completion, hints, and \f[CR]man\f[R]
search.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]NO_COLOR\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A nonempty value disables color in the prompt, diagnostics, the editor,
and the bundled utilities.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]OPTERR\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A value of 0 silences \f[CR]getopts\f[R] diagnostics.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]PATH\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The colon\-separated command search path.
An empty element, an empty value, and an unset PATH search the current
directory.
The shell does not set PATH when the environment lacks it.
On Windows the separator is a semicolon, an empty element is skipped,
and Path counts as PATH.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]PROMPT_COMMAND, PS0, PS1, PS2, PS3, PS4, RPS1, RPROMPT\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The prompts and the command run before each prompt, as described under
Prompting.
PS3 is the prompt of \f[CR]select\f[R], \[dq]#?
\[dq] by default.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]SHELL\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The path of the user shell.
An inherited value is kept, and an unset SHELL is set to the path of the
executable.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]TERM, COLORTERM, TERM_PROGRAM\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Terminal capabilities, as described under Line editing.
TERM=dumb disables color and the terminal integration marks.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]TIMEFORMAT\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The format of the \f[CR]time\f[R] report.
%R, %U, and %S are the elapsed, user, and system seconds, %P is the
processor percentage, %3R selects three decimals, %lR selects minutes
and seconds, and %% is a percent sign.
An empty value prints nothing, and an unset value selects the default
report.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]TMPDIR\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The directory for the temporary files of \f[CR]fc\f[R], Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E,
and \f[CR]slurp\f[R], read from the environment, so an assignment needs
\f[CR]export\f[R].
An empty value is ignored, and /tmp is the default.
On Windows the directory is TEMP.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]TZ\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The time zone.
The shell uses the value it inherited, so an assignment in the shell
changes the zone of later programs only.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The base of the user settings directory, as described under Startup
files.
A relative or empty value is ignored.
.PP
These Bash variables have no effect.
They are HISTCONTROL, HISTFILE, HISTFILESIZE, HISTIGNORE, HISTSIZE,
HISTTIMEFORMAT, histchars, MAIL, MAILCHECK, MAILPATH, TMOUT,
PROMPT_DIRTRIM, FIGNORE, EXECIGNORE, HOSTFILE, INPUTRC, CHILD_MAX,
BASH_COMPAT, BASH_LOADABLES_PATH, GLOBSORT, FUNCNEST, auto_resume, and
POSIXLY_CORRECT.
.SS Arrays
An indexed array is a list of values with integer indices from 0, and an
associative array maps string keys to values.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood a subscript assignment creates an element, a
compound assignment assigns an empty string, and unset rejects a
subscript.
.PP
a=(x y z) assigns an indexed array, a=([2]=c [0]=a) assigns explicit
indices, a[2]=z assigns an element, and a+=(w) appends.
Elements may be missing, so a[5]=z on a two\-element array leaves
indices 0, 1, and 5.
${a[i]} takes an arithmetic subscript, and a negative index counts from
the end.
.PP
A scalar variable acts as element 0, and ${a} is ${a[0]}.
${a[\[at]]} and ${a[*]} list elements, ${#a[\[at]]} counts them, and
${!a[\[at]]} lists the indices that are set.
\[dq]${a[\[at]]}\[dq] keeps one field per element,
\[dq]${a[\[at]]\[at]A}\[dq] yields the words of the declaration,
\[dq]${a[\[at]]\[at]K}\[dq] yields keys and values, and
\[dq]${a[*]}\[dq] joins with the first byte of IFS.
An unquoted ${a[\[at]]} or ${a[*]} joins with the first byte of IFS and
splits the result.
.PP
Under \f[CR]set \-u\f[R], ${a[\[at]]} and ${a[*]} of an unset array
expand to nothing, and $a and ${a[0]} are errors.
\f[CR]unset \[aq]a[i]\[aq]\f[R] removes one element.
.PP
\f[CR]declare \-A m\f[R] declares an associative array, m[key]=v assigns
an element, ${m[key]} expands it, ${!m[\[at]]} lists keys, and
\f[CR]unset \[aq]m[key]\[aq]\f[R] removes one.
A key is a string, so m[1+1] holds the key 1+1.
Keys are listed in the order in which they were first assigned.
Bash can list them in another order, so a script that depends on the
order should sort the keys.
.PP
\f[CR]export\f[R] and \f[CR]declare \-x\f[R] on an array keep the array
and record the export attribute.
\f[CR]declare \-p\f[R] prints the attribute as \-ax or \-Ax, and no
child receives the array.
.SS Expansions
The shell expands each word of a command in a fixed order.
Brace expansion comes first, then tilde expansion, parameter expansion,
arithmetic expansion, command substitution, and process substitution
from left to right, and then field splitting, pathname expansion, and
quote removal.
Only brace expansion, field splitting, and pathname expansion can change
the number of words.
.SS Brace expansion
A word holding an unquoted {\f[I]a\f[R],\f[I]b\f[R],...} produces one
word per item, each with the text before and after the braces, as in
a{b,c}d giving abd and acd.
Items may be empty and braces nest.
A sequence {\f[I]x\f[R]..\f[I]y\f[R]} or
{\f[I]x\f[R]..\f[I]y\f[R]..\f[I]step\f[R]} produces integers or single
characters from x to y.
A leading zero on either integer pads every result to the same width, a
step of 0 counts as 1, and the sign of the step is ignored.
.PP
A character range includes every character between, as in {Z..a}.
A word that is not a valid list or sequence, such as {a}, {a\-z}, or
{1..a}, stays literal, and so does a sequence that holds an expansion.
Brace expansion does not apply in an assignment value, a here\-string, a
case pattern, or a redirection target.
It is off in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood and while \f[CR]braceexpand\f[R] is
off.
.SS Tilde expansion
An unquoted \[ti] at the start of a word expands to HOME,
\[ti]\f[I]user\f[R] to the home directory of user, \[ti]+ to PWD, and
\[ti]\- to OLDPWD.
The prefix ends at the first slash.
An unknown user, \[ti]\- with OLDPWD unset, and the directory stack
forms \[ti]N, \[ti]+N, and \[ti]\-N stay literal.
An empty HOME expands to an empty word, and an unset HOME is an error.
.PP
A tilde also expands after = and : in an assignment and in the words of
\f[CR]export\f[R], \f[CR]declare\f[R], \f[CR]local\f[R], and
\f[CR]readonly\f[R], and outside the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood in any argument
that looks like an assignment, such as a=\[ti].
.SS Parameter expansion
$\f[I]name\f[R] and ${\f[I]name\f[R]} expand to the value of a
parameter.
The braces are needed for a positional parameter above 9, an array
element, or a name followed by a character that could continue it.
In the forms below, a colon also tests for an empty value, and without
the colon only an unset parameter counts.
The word is expanded only when it is used.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name:\-word}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The value, or word when the parameter is unset or empty.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name:=word}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The value, after assigning word when the parameter is unset or empty.
Positional and special parameters cannot be assigned this way.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name:?word}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The value, or an error message holding word when the parameter is unset
or empty.
A non\-interactive shell exits.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name:+word}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Word when the parameter is set and not empty, and nothing otherwise.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${#name}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The length of the value in characters.
${#*} and ${#\[at]} give the number of positional parameters.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name:offset}, ${name:offset:length}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A substring.
A negative offset counts from the end and needs a leading space, as in
${v: \-3}, since ${v:\-3} is the default\-value form.
A negative length excludes that many characters from the end.
On an indexed array, ${a[\[at]]:offset:length} starts at the first
element whose index is at least the offset.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name#pattern}, ${name##pattern}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The value with the shortest or the longest prefix matching pattern
removed.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name%pattern}, ${name%%pattern}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The value with the shortest or the longest suffix matching pattern
removed.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name/pattern/string}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The value with the first match of pattern replaced by string.
// replaces every match, and /# and /% anchor the match at the start and
the end.
The \f[CR]patsub_replacement\f[R] option is on by default.
While it is on, an unquoted ampersand in the replacement, or one from an
unquoted expansion, stands for the match, and a quoted ampersand or
\[rs]& is literal.
With the option off, every ampersand is literal.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name\[ha]pattern}, ${name\[ha]\[ha]pattern}, ${name,pattern}, ${name,,pattern}, ${name\[ti]pattern}, ${name\[ti]\[ti]pattern}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Convert the first character, or every character, that matches pattern to
upper case, lower case, or the other case.
Without a pattern, every character matches.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${name\[at]op}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Transform the value.
Q quotes it for reuse, E expands backslash escapes as $\[aq]...\[aq]
does, P expands it as a prompt, A gives an assignment or declaration, a
gives the attribute letters, U, L, and u convert to upper case, lower
case, or an upper\-case first character, and K and k give the keys and
values of an array.
Any other operator on a set parameter is a bad substitution.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood has no transformations.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${!name}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The value of the variable whose identifier is the value of name.
For a name reference it gives the identifier of the target.
An unset or empty name gives an empty value.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${!prefix*}, ${!prefix\[at]}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The identifiers of the variables whose identifiers begin with prefix.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]${!name[\[at]]}, ${!name[*]}\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The indices or keys of an array.
.PP
A value or key with a byte that does not print in the locale, such as a
control byte or invalid UTF\-8, takes the $\[aq]...\[aq] form in
${v\[at]Q}, ${v\[at]A}, ${a[\[at]]\[at]K}, printf %q, declare \-p, and
export \-p.
.PP
Inside double quotes or a here\-document, the word of ${v:\-word},
${v:+word}, and ${v:=word}, with or without the colon, follows
double\-quote rules, so \[dq]${v:\-\[aq]a\[aq]}\[dq] expands to
\[aq]a\[aq] with its quotes.
.SS Command substitution
$(\f[I]command\f[R]) and \[ga]\f[I]command\f[R]\[ga] expand to the
output of the command with trailing newlines removed.
Inside backquotes, a backslash keeps its meaning only before $, \[ga],
or \[rs].
$(< \f[I]file\f[R]) expands to the contents of file without running a
command.
The Bash and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods drop null bytes from the output, and
the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood keeps them.
.PP
The command runs in a subshell, so the shell state, options, directory
stack, working directory, and file creation mask of the shell are
unchanged after it finishes.
.PP
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R], \f[CR]bash\f[R], and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] moods
also accept two forms that run in the shell.
${ \f[I]command\f[R]; } expands to the output of the command, and ${|
\f[I]command\f[R]; } expands to the value of REPLY after the command.
Variable changes in both forms remain.
.PP
A syntax error inside a substitution is reported at its place in the
outer source, and the script or command string holding it does not run.
Nesting deeper than 64 levels is a syntax error.
.SS Arithmetic expansion
$((\f[I]expression\f[R])) expands to the value of the expression,
evaluated as described under Arithmetic evaluation.
The expression undergoes parameter expansion, command substitution, and
quote removal first.
An empty $(( )) yields 0.
Outside the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, $[\f[I]expression\f[R]] is an obsolete
spelling of the same expansion.
.SS Process substitution
<(\f[I]command\f[R]) and >(\f[I]command\f[R]) expand to a path from
which the output of the command can be read, or to which its input can
be written, with the command in a separate process.
The form expands anywhere in an unquoted word, so p=<(command) assigns
the path, and every mood accepts it.
The path stays open until the command that expanded it finishes, or
until the loop finishes for a path in the word list of a for loop.
.PP
The shell then closes it and waits for the process.
A process substitution sets $!
to its process, and \f[CR]wait\f[R] on that process returns its status.
On POSIX the path is /dev/fd/63 or the next free descriptor above it.
On Windows it is a pipe that acts as a FIFO, so each reader continues
where the previous one stopped, and each writer appends to the same
stream.
.SS Field splitting
The results of unquoted parameter expansion, command substitution, and
arithmetic expansion are split into fields at the bytes of IFS.
A run of IFS whitespace collapses and is ignored at the start and the
end, and each other IFS byte delimits a field, and that field may be
empty.
An empty IFS disables splitting.
An unquoted expansion that yields nothing produces no field, and
\[dq]\[dq] produces an empty field.
.SS Pathname expansion
After field splitting, each unquoted word holding *, ?, or [ is a
pattern, and the shell replaces it with the sorted list of matching
pathnames, as described under Pattern matching.
A leading dot and a slash must match explicitly, and .
and ..
are never results.
The Bash moods sort matches by the collation order of the locale, and
the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods by byte value.
\f[CR]noglob\f[R] disables pathname expansion.
.PP
\f[CR]dotglob\f[R] lets * and ?
match a leading dot, \f[CR]nocaseglob\f[R] ignores case, and
\f[CR]globstar\f[R] makes ** match any number of directories, with **/
matching directories only.
A pattern that matches nothing is an error in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood.
In the other moods it stays literal, \f[CR]nullglob\f[R] removes it, and
\f[CR]failglob\f[R] makes it an error.
.PP
A nonempty \f[CR]GLOBIGNORE\f[R] holds colon\-separated patterns.
A result matching one is removed, and a pattern whose results are all
removed follows the rules for a pattern that matches nothing.
Assigning a nonempty GLOBIGNORE enables \f[CR]dotglob\f[R], and
unsetting it disables \f[CR]dotglob\f[R].
.SS Pattern matching
Pathname expansion, case, the == and != operators of [[ ]], and the
pattern forms of parameter expansion use these patterns.
* matches any string, ?
matches one character, and [...]
matches one character from a set.
A set may hold ranges such as a\-z and the classes [:alnum:], [:alpha:],
[:blank:], [:cntrl:], [:digit:], [:graph:], [:lower:], [:print:],
[:punct:], [:space:], [:upper:], and [:xdigit:].
.PP
A leading !
or \[ha] negates the set, and ] first in the set is literal.
Equivalence classes and collating symbols match nothing, and so do
reversed ranges.
An unclosed [ is literal.
A backslash or quotes make a character literal.
Outside pathname expansion, * and ?
also match a slash and a leading dot.
.PP
While \f[CR]extglob\f[R] is on, ?(\f[I]list\f[R]), *(\f[I]list\f[R]),
+(\f[I]list\f[R]), \[at](\f[I]list\f[R]), and !(\f[I]list\f[R]) match
zero or one, zero or more, one or more, exactly one, or anything except
one of the |\-separated patterns.
The pattern of [[ ]] always accepts them.
\f[CR]nocasematch\f[R] makes case, [[ ]], and =\[ti] ignore case.
.SS Quote removal
After the other expansions, unquoted backslashes, single quotes, and
double quotes that did not result from an expansion are removed.
.SS Redirections
A redirection opens, duplicates, or closes a descriptor for a command.
Redirections are processed from left to right.
A number before the operator selects the descriptor, and a
{\f[I]variable\f[R]} before it allocates a descriptor numbered 10 or
above and stores the number in the variable.
The variable may be an array element, and the number stays in the
variable after the descriptor closes.
.PP
The target word undergoes the same expansions as an argument except
pathname expansion of more than one result.
A target that expands to several words is an error, and so is an unset
variable in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood.
.PP
A failed redirection fails the command with status 1 in the Bash moods
and 2 in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods.
On a special builtin or a compound command it ends the script in the
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood with status 1 and in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood with
status 2, and the Bash moods continue, including \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R],
where Bash exits.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]<word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Open word for reading on n, by default 0.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]>word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Open word for writing on n, by default 1, creating it or truncating it.
While \f[CR]noclobber\f[R] is on, an existing regular file is refused.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]>|word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Open word for writing, even under \f[CR]noclobber\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]>>word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Open word for appending.
An appending redirection inside a loop body opens its file once for the
whole loop, so writes continue to the original file even after it is
renamed or removed.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]<>word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Open word for reading and writing on n, by default 0.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]&>word, >&word, &>>word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Redirect standard output and standard error to word, or append both.
With >&, a word that is a number, or \-, duplicates or closes standard
output, and &> and &>> always open a file.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]<&m, [n]>&m\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Duplicate descriptor m onto n.
A target that is not a single open descriptor is an error.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]<&\-, [n]>&\-\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Close n.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]<&m\-, [n]>&m\-\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Move m onto n and close m.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]<<word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A here\-document.
The lines after the command, up to a line that is exactly word, become
the input.
When any part of word is quoted, the lines are taken literally.
Otherwise they undergo parameter expansion, command substitution, and
arithmetic expansion, a backslash keeps its meaning only before $,
\[ga], \[rs], or a newline, and a backslash before a newline joins
lines.
A command can have several here\-documents, read in order.
An unterminated here\-document ends at the end of the input.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]<<\-word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A here\-document whose lines and terminator lose their leading tabs.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB][n]<<<word\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A here\-string.
The expanded word, followed by a newline, becomes the input.
.PP
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood rejects &>, &>>, >&\f[I]word\f[R], and <<<.
Device paths such as /dev/tcp are not emulated, so a redirection to one
opens the path in the file system.
.PP
In the Bash moods, the redirections of a program, of a command with no
command word, and of a subshell expand in the child, so a side effect
such as ${v:=x} does not reach the shell.
Those of a builtin, a function, a group, and \f[CR]exec\f[R] expand in
the shell, as do those of every command in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and
\f[CR]sh\f[R] moods.
.SS Aliases
An alias replaces the first word of a simple command with a string.
The replacement is expanded when the command runs, so an alias defined
earlier on the same line or in the same compound command applies at
once, and an alias in a function body takes its value at each call.
When the replacement ends with a blank, the next word is also checked
for an alias.
.PP
A chain of aliases is followed, and a word that is already being
replaced is not replaced again.
A quoted word, a word with a backslash, and a word produced by an
expansion are never aliases, and a reserved word keeps its meaning.
.PP
Aliases expand while \f[CR]expand_aliases\f[R] is on.
It is on in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R], \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], and
\f[CR]sh\f[R] moods, and in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood it is on in an
interactive shell and off in a script.
\f[CR]alias\f[R] and \f[CR]unalias\f[R] manage them, and BASH_ALIASES
holds them.
.SS Functions
A function runs in the shell, with its own positional parameters.
Its variables are global unless \f[CR]local\f[R] or \f[CR]declare\f[R]
makes them local, and a local variable is visible to the functions it
calls.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods, local without a value
keeps the outer value visible, and in the Bash moods the variable starts
unset.
.PP
\f[CR]return\f[R] ends the function with a status, and otherwise the
status is that of the last command.
FUNCNAME, BASH_SOURCE, BASH_LINENO, and BASH_COMMAND expose the call
stack, and $0 stays the script name.
Function calls nest at most 900 deep and sourced files 400 deep, or less
when the stack runs short, and a deeper call fails with a diagnostic.
.PP
A function called from a guarded and\-or operand or a negation keeps
\f[CR]errexit\f[R] suppressed throughout its body.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood the RETURN trap fires at the end of every
function body.
The \f[CR]bash\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] moods fire it for a body
that \f[CR]functrace\f[R] follows, and the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood rejects
the RETURN condition.
.PP
\f[CR]export \-f\f[R] exports a function to the environment as
BASH_FUNC_\f[I]name\f[R]%%, which Bash children import.
Koshka does not import exported functions.
.SS Arithmetic evaluation
Arithmetic expressions appear in $(( )), (( )), let, the C\-style for
loop, array subscripts, substring offsets, and assignments to integer
variables.
The operators, from the highest precedence to the lowest, are
