.TE
.PP
Parentheses group.
A comparison or logical operator yields 1 or 0, and && and || evaluate
their right side only when needed.
**= is a Koshka extension.
.PP
A number is decimal, octal with a leading 0, hexadecimal with 0x, binary
with 0b, which is a Koshka extension, or in base \f[I]b\f[R] from 2 to
64 as \f[I]b\f[R]#\f[I]digits\f[R], with digits beyond 9 taken from
letters, \[at], and _.
A variable is replaced by its value, and the value is evaluated as an
expression in turn, so x=1+2 makes x*2 equal 6.
.PP
An empty value counts as 0.
A $ or a backquote inside a variable value is an error.
A subscript taken from a value is expanded again, so with
q=\[aq]a[$i]\[aq], $((q)) is the element at i.
Outside the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, double quotes inside $(( )), (( )), a
C\-style for header, a subscript, or a substring offset are removed
first, so $(( \[dq]1 + 2\[dq] * 3 )) is 7.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood rejects every quote there.
.PP
In the \f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods,
arithmetic uses signed 64\-bit integers that wrap, and an unset variable
counts as 0.
The \f[CR]interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers\f[R] option, held on
in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, selects arbitrary\-precision integers and
finite decimal values, so $((2**100)) is exact and $((7.0/2)) is 3.5.
.PP
Division of two integers stays integer division.
Shifts, bitwise operators, and the exponent need integers, and a
negative shift count is an error.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood an unset variable in an expression is an
error.
.PP
Division by zero, an invalid digit, a missing operand, and an unexpected
token are errors, as described under Shell errors.
.SS Conditional expressions
The [[ ]] command and the \f[CR]test\f[R] and [ builtins evaluate
conditional expressions.
Inside [[ ]] the words do not undergo field splitting or pathname
expansion.
The primaries are
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-a file, \-e file\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The file exists.
[[ ]] accepts \-a as a unary operator, and test does not.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-b file, \-c file, \-d file, \-f file, \-p file, \-S file\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The file is a block device, a character device, a directory, a regular
file, a FIFO, or a socket.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-h file, \-L file\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The file is a symbolic link.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-g file, \-u file, \-k file\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The file has the set\-group\-id, set\-user\-id, or sticky bit.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-r file, \-w file, \-x file\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The file is readable, writable, or executable by the shell.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-O file, \-G file\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The file is owned by the effective user or group.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-s file\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The file is a regular file with a nonzero size.
A directory is false.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-t fd\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The descriptor is open on a terminal.
[ \-t ] with no operand is true.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-z string, \-n string\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The string is empty, or not empty.
A lone string is true when it is not empty.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-v name\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The variable, array element, or positional parameter is set.
Only [[ ]] accepts \-v.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-R name\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The variable is a name reference with a target.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood does not accept \-R.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\-o option\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] option is on.
[[ ]] accepts \-o in every mood, and test only in the Bash moods.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]file1 \-ef file2\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The two files are the same file.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]file1 \-nt file2, file1 \-ot file2\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
file1 is newer or older than file2.
Both files must exist.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]s1 == s2, s1 = s2, s1 != s2\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The strings are equal or differ.
In [[ ]] the right side is a pattern unless it is quoted.
test accepts == only in the Bash moods.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]s1 < s2, s1 > s2\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
s1 sorts before or after s2.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]s =\[ti] regex\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
In [[ ]] only, s matches the extended regular expression.
A quoted part of the regex is literal.
BASH_REMATCH receives the match and its groups, an unmatched group is
empty, and a dot matches a newline.
An invalid regex is an error with status 1, where Bash returns 2.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]n1 \-eq n2, \-ne, \-lt, \-le, \-gt, \-ge\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Integer comparisons.
In [[ ]] each operand is an arithmetic expression.
In test each operand must be a decimal integer, which may have blanks
around it.
.PP
\f[CR]!\f[R] \f[I]expr\f[R] negates, ( ) groups, and && and || combine
inside [[ ]].
test uses \-a and \-o for and and or, and its outcome depends on the
number of arguments, as POSIX specifies.
A malformed [[ ]] fails with status 2.
.SS Command execution
After a simple command is expanded, the shell looks for the command word
in this order: functions, enabled builtins, cached program paths, PATH,
and, when the mood, the options, or \f[CR]enable\f[R] allow them,
bundled utilities.
A word holding a slash runs that path directly.
Running a command or \f[CR]hash\f[R] caches its path.
A cached path stays until PATH is assigned, \f[CR]hash \-r\f[R] runs, or
a directory change affects a relative PATH entry.
.PP
A program runs in a separate process with the exported variables as its
environment.
A command that is not found returns 127, and a file that cannot be run
returns 126.
A command path ending in a directory separator fails with status 126
when the target is not a directory.
A file without a recognized executable format runs as a shell script in
a new shell, without the variables, functions, and traps of the caller.
.PP
In an interactive shell, a single directory path in command position
changes to that directory as \f[CR]cd\f[R] would, unless a function,
builtin, program, or bundled utility of that name exists.
A word of only dots moves up one directory per dot after the first.
The \f[CR]autocd\f[R] option controls this and is on in every mood, and
scripts are never affected.
.SS Command execution environment
The shell has an execution environment that consists of the open files,
the working directory, the file creation mask, the resource limits, the
traps, the variables and their attributes, the functions, the options,
the aliases, the directory stack, and the job table.
.PP
A subshell, a command substitution, a pipeline stage that runs in its
own process, and an asynchronous list receive a copy of this
environment, and their changes do not reach the shell.
Traps that the shell catches are reset to their defaults in them, and
traps that the shell ignores stay ignored.
A subshell cannot wait for a job of its parent.
.PP
A program receives the open descriptors, the working directory, the
creation mask, the resource limits, the ignored signals, and the
exported variables.
Every descriptor the shell opens for itself is closed in the program.
.SS Environment
At startup the shell imports the variables of its environment and marks
them for export.
A variable inherited this way, and one marked with \f[CR]export\f[R] or
\f[CR]declare \-x\f[R], is passed to every program the shell starts.
A prefix assignment adds a variable for one command.
The values that the shell maintains, such as SECONDS, IFS, SHELLOPTS,
and the KOSH variables, are not taken from the environment.
.PP
On Windows variable names are not case\-sensitive, and a value holds at
most 32,766 UTF\-16 units, so a child receives a longer value truncated.
Variables whose names begin with KOSH_INTERNAL_ are reserved.
.SS Shell errors
A syntax error in a script, a \f[CR]\-c\f[R] string, an eval, or a
sourced file keeps all of it from running, and the status is 2.
The parser reports every syntax error in one run.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, analysis can also reject a script before it
runs, with status 1, as described under Diagnostics.
An interactive shell rejects the line and reads the next one.
.PP
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood every arithmetic and expansion error ends
the script with status 1.
A usage error of a builtin, such as an unknown option, ends the script
with the status of the error, except for declare, trap, getopts,
compopt, and printf.
With valid usage, a failure of bg, cd, dirs, fg, kill, let, local,
mapfile, popd, pushd, read, set, shift, test, ulimit, umask, or z also
ends the script, and a failure of another builtin, such as a missing
alias, only sets its status.
.PP
A failed redirection of a special builtin or a compound command ends the
script with status 1, and a write error on standard output, an invalid
regular expression, and a malformed [[ ]] also end it.
.PP
In the \f[CR]bash\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] moods an arithmetic
error in $(( )), a subscript, a substring offset, or an assignment to an
integer variable abandons the rest of the input line, as in Bash.
The remaining commands on the line do not run, $?
is 1 on the next line, and neither \f[CR]set \-e\f[R] nor an ERR trap
acts on it.
.PP
An eval, a sourced file, a subshell, and a command substitution count as
separate lines.
An error in (( )), \f[CR]let\f[R], a [[ ]] operand, or a C\-style for
header fails only that command with status 1.
Outside a here\-document body, a $(( )) error ends a non\-interactive
shell in the \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] mood, with status 127 for a
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] string and 1 for a script, and in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood
with status 2.
An interactive shell abandons the line.
.PP
Other expansion errors follow Bash 5.3.
A glob matching nothing under failglob abandons the line in the
\f[CR]bash\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] moods.
A bad substitution, such as ${x!y} or ${x:}, and an assignment to a
read\-only variable abandon the line in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood and end
a non\-interactive shell in the \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R]
moods.
An unknown transformation such as ${v\[at]Z}, an unset parameter under
\f[CR]set \-u\f[R], and ${name?word} end the script in every mood.
.PP
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] moods, a syntax error in
an eval string or a sourced file ends a non\-interactive shell with
status 2, and the other moods fail only that command.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, an error of a regular builtin, such as a
missing cd directory or an invalid test operand, fails that command with
status 2 and the script goes on.
.PP
An error of a special builtin or of local, such as shift past the last
parameter or export of an invalid identifier, ends a non\-interactive
shell with status 2 unless \f[CR]command\f[R] runs it.
A failed exec ends a non\-interactive shell in every mood.
.PP
An error that ends a non\-interactive shell passes through an eval and a
sourced file, as in Bash and dash.
A subshell, a command substitution, a trap action, and a startup file
contain it, and the command that started them receives the status.
.SS Exit status
The status of a command is a number from 0 to 255.
Zero means success and any other value means failure.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]0\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Success, and the status of an empty command, of a compound command that
ran no list, and of an asynchronous command.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]1\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A general failure, such as a false condition, a failed builtin, or
analysis that rejected a script.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]2\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A usage error of a builtin or the shell, or a syntax error.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]126\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A command that was found but cannot be run, such as a directory, a file
without execute permission, or a path ending in a separator that is not
a directory.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]127\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A command that was not found, or a script operand that cannot be opened.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]128+n\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A command ended by signal n, such as 130 for SIGINT, 141 for SIGPIPE,
and 143 for SIGTERM.
.PP
The shell exits with the status of the last command it ran, or with the
operand of \f[CR]exit\f[R], taken modulo 256.
When an expansion error ends a non\-interactive shell, the status is 1
in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood and 2 in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
In the Bash moods an unset parameter under nounset, ${name:?}, and a bad
substitution that ends the shell return 127 from a \f[CR]\-c\f[R]
string, as in Bash, and 1 from a script.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R]
return 0 when every input succeeds, 1 when an input cannot be read,
parsed, formatted, or replaced or analysis reports an error, and 2 for
an invalid option combination.
Warning\-only findings return 0.
.SS Signals
The shell ignores SIGPIPE, and a program it starts receives the default,
so a producer killed by SIGPIPE returns status 141.
An interactive shell also ignores SIGTERM, SIGQUIT, SIGTSTP, and
SIGTTIN.
SIGINT interrupts the running command or the line being edited and
reports \f[CR]Interrupted\f[R], with status 130.
.PP
A shell that does not trap SIGHUP, and a non\-interactive shell that
does not trap SIGTERM or SIGQUIT, is terminated by the signal, so its
parent sees status 128 plus the signal number.
.PP
When a signal ends a command, the shell prints a line such as
\f[CR][Process 2585: Killed, signal 9]\f[R] on standard error, once for
each killed pipeline stage, and the status is 128 plus the signal
number.
SIGPIPE and SIGINT print no such line.
.PP
\f[CR]trap\f[R] sets the action of a signal or of the conditions EXIT,
ERR, DEBUG, and RETURN.
A trapped signal runs its action at the next command boundary.
The CHLD trap runs once for every child the shell reaps, and under
\f[CR]set \-m\f[R] a job that stops counts too.
When a trapped signal arrives while \f[CR]wait\f[R] blocks,
\f[CR]wait\f[R] returns 128 plus the signal number and the job keeps
running.
On Windows a background process runs in its own console process group.
.PP
As process 1, the shell reaps orphaned children it did not start, before
each command and while it waits.
On SIGTERM it sends SIGTERM and then SIGCONT to every other process,
interrupts the running command, waits for every remaining child, and
exits with status 143.
.SS Job control
A job is a pipeline or list that the shell runs in the background, or a
foreground pipeline that was stopped.
Every stage of an asynchronous pipeline belongs to one job, and POSIX
stages share a process group.
The final stage determines $!, the status, and the job identity, except
that under pipefail the rightmost failing stage may determine the
status.
.PP
A stopped stage is recorded even when it is not the last, and
\f[CR]fg\f[R] and \f[CR]bg\f[R] resume every stage.
Job control is on in an interactive shell, where Ctrl\-Z stops the
foreground job.
.PP
A job is referred to by a job spec.
%\f[I]n\f[R] is job n, %+, %%, and % are the current job, and %\- is the
previous one.
%\f[I]string\f[R] is the job whose command begins with string, and
%?\f[I]string\f[R] the job whose command contains it.
When several jobs match, the lowest\-numbered one is used.
A bare number is also accepted as a job number.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood an unquoted %?string is a glob, so it needs
quotes.
.PP
\f[CR]jobs\f[R] and job notifications print one line per job, as in
Bash.
The line holds the job number with a + for the current job or a \- for
the previous one, the process with \f[CR]jobs \-l\f[R], the state, and
the command.
A job ended by a signal shows its description, such as Terminated, and
an exited job shows Done, or Exit and its status.
An interactive shell reports a job that finished or changed state before
the next prompt.
.PP
A job reported by \f[CR]wait\f[R], \f[CR]jobs\f[R], or a notification
leaves the job table, and a later \f[CR]wait\f[R] on its process returns
the remembered status.
The shell remembers the 1024 most recent statuses, and a \f[CR]wait\f[R]
without operands discards them.
A subshell, a pipeline stage, an asynchronous command, and a coprocess
cannot wait for a job of their parent.
.PP
The shell never sends SIGHUP to its jobs, so running background jobs
continue after it exits, and it exits even while jobs are stopped.
\f[CR]disown\f[R] removes a job from the table.
.SS History expansion
In an interactive shell, while the \f[CR]histexpand\f[R] option is on,
which is the default, an unquoted !
in a line starts a history reference.
The shell replaces the reference before it parses the line.
A non\-interactive shell does not expand history.
A reference has an event, an optional word designator, and optional
modifiers.
.PP
The line with its references replaced is printed before it runs.
History expansion takes place inside double quotes, and a single\-quoted
or backslash\-escaped !
stays literal.
.SS Event designators
\f[B]\f[CB]!!\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The previous event.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]!n\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Event number n.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]!\-n\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The event n before the current one.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]!string\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The newest event that begins with string.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]!?string?\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The newest event that contains string.
The closing ?
may be omitted at the end of the line.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]!#\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The current line up to this reference.
It takes no word designators or modifiers.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]\[ha]old\[ha]new\[ha]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
At the start of a line, the previous event with the first old replaced
by new, as !!:s/old/new/ does.
.SS Word designators
A colon separates a word designator from the event, and it may be
omitted before \[ha], $, and *.
Words are counted from 0, the command.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:\[ha], :$, :*\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The first argument, the last argument, and every argument.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:n\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Word n.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:n\-m, :\-m, :n*, :n\-$\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Words n through m, words 0 through m, words n through the last, and
words n through the last.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:n\-\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Words n through the next to last.
.PP
!$, !\[ha], and !* are the last argument, the first argument, and every
argument of the previous event.
.SS Modifiers
Each modifier follows a colon.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:h, :t, :r, :e\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Remove the last path component, keep only the last component, remove a
trailing suffix, or keep only the suffix.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:s/old/new/\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Replace the first occurrence of old, as literal text.
Another delimiter may replace the slash, and the last delimiter may be
omitted at the end of the line.
An & in new stands for old.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:gs, :as\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Replace every occurrence.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:Gs\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Replace the first occurrence in each word.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:&\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Repeat the last substitution.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:q, :x\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Quote the text as one argument, or each word separately.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]:p\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the result and record it in the history without running it.
.PP
A reference that matches no event, a substitution that finds no match, a
missing substitution text, and an unknown designator are errors, and the
command neither runs nor enters the history.
.SS Builtins
A builtin runs inside the shell.
The special builtins are :, ., break, continue, eval, exec, exit,
export, readonly, return, set, shift, times, trap, and unset, and in the
Bash moods source is also special.
Assignments before a special builtin remain in the shell, as described
under Simple commands, and their errors follow the rules under Shell
errors.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood hides let and time.
.PP
A builtin given \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] before its other arguments prints
its description, synopsis, and options.
In the compatibility moods echo prints \-\-help and test ignores it, and
[ \-\-help reports a missing ] in every mood.
Unless an entry states otherwise, a builtin returns 0 on success, 1 on a
failure, and 2 for an unknown option or a wrong number of operands.
.SS \f[CR]:\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]: [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]true [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Do nothing and return 0.
The arguments are expanded and ignored, except that \-\-help prints the
help.
.SS \f[CR].\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]. [\-\-mood[=]MOOD] file [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]source [\-\-mood[=]MOOD] file [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Read and run the commands of file in the shell, and return the status of
the last one, or the operand of a \f[CR]return\f[R] in the file.
The arguments become the positional parameters while the file runs, and
the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood ignores them.
The \f[CR]sourcepath\f[R] option is on by default.
While it is on, a file without a slash is searched in PATH and then in
the current directory.
A missing or unreadable file returns 1, and in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood it
ends a non\-interactive shell with status 2.
.PP
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, a Bash startup file (.bashrc,
\&.bash_profile, .bash_login, .bash_logout, .profile, or /etc/profile)
runs in the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood with its options, and a file whose
shebang selects another shell runs in that mood.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood returns when the file ends, and the variables,
functions, and aliases it defined stay.
.PP
Analysis does not follow such a file, a syntax error in it fails the
command without ending the shell, and the files it sources keep its
mood.
A file without a shebang runs in the current mood.
\f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R] runs file in MOOD in place of the mood its shebang
or name selects, and restores the previous mood afterwards.
.SS \f[CR][\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB][ expression ]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]test expression\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate a conditional expression, as described under Conditional
expressions, and return 0 when it is true and 1 when it is false.
No expression is false.
A malformed expression, a missing ], and an operand of an integer
comparison that is not a decimal integer return 2.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods == is rejected with a
hint to use =.
.SS \f[CR]alias\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]alias [name[=value] ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Define an alias for each name with a value, and print the definition of
each name without one.
Without operands, print every alias as an alias command.
An unknown alias or an empty name returns 1.
A name may hold any character except =.
.SS \f[CR]bg\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]bg [job]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Resume a stopped job in the background, by default the current job.
A missing job returns 1, or 2 in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.SS \f[CR]bind\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]bind [options] [keyseq:function ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Accept the readline options \-lmrvpPsq, \-f, and \-u and their operands
without changing key bindings, and return 0.
The key bindings are described under Line editing.
.SS \f[CR]break\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]break [n]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]continue [n]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Leave the innermost n enclosing loops, by default 1, or start their next
iteration.
A count above the nesting depth applies to the outermost loop.
A count of 0 returns 1 and leaves every enclosing loop in the Bash
moods, as Bash does, and the innermost loop in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood it returns 2 and ends a non\-interactive
shell.
.PP
A count that is not a number ends a non\-interactive shell with status 2
in every mood.
Outside a loop the commands do nothing.
A loop in a calling function is not affected.
.SS \f[CR]builtin\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]builtin [name [argument ...]]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]builtin \-\-list\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run the builtin name even when a function of that name exists.
A name that is not a builtin returns 1.
\f[CR]\-\-list\f[R] prints every builtin, one per line.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, builtin with no operand lists the builtins
under POSIX, BASH, and Koshka headings.
.SS \f[CR]caller\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]caller [n]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the line, function, and file of call frame n, counting from 0 for
the caller of the current function.
When n is past the last frame, caller returns 1.
Without an operand, caller prints the line and file of the caller inside
a function, a sourced file, or a script file, where the top level prints
0 NULL, and returns 1 in a command string or standard input.
An operand that is not a whole number returns 2, and a second operand is
ignored.
.SS \f[CR]cd\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]cd [\-L|\-P] [dir]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Change the working directory to dir, by default HOME, and set PWD and
OLDPWD.
An operand of \- changes to OLDPWD and prints it.
A relative dir that does not begin with .
or ..
is searched in each CDPATH directory, and a directory found there is
printed.
A relative directory resolves against the logical PWD, and ..
removes its last component, unless \f[CR]\-P\f[R] or the
\f[CR]physical\f[R] option resolves symbolic links first.
.PP
\f[CR]\-L\f[R] keeps the logical path.
A missing directory and an unset HOME or OLDPWD return 1, and 2 in the
\f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, and more than one operand returns 2.
In the Bash moods an empty operand returns 1, and the other moods accept
it.
A restricted shell refuses cd.
.SS \f[CR]command\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]command [\-pVv] name [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run name as a builtin or program, skipping functions and aliases.
A special builtin run this way loses its special properties.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] searches the default PATH.
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] prints the path, builtin, keyword, function, or alias
definition that name resolves to, and \f[CR]\-V\f[R] describes it in
words.
With \-v or \-V the status is 0 when any operand resolves, and 1 when
none does, or 127 in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
Running a command that is not found returns 127, and one that cannot be
run 126.
.SS \f[CR]compgen\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]compgen [options] [word]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the candidates that the options generate and that begin with word,
one per line.
The actions \-a (aliases), \-b (builtins), \-c (commands), \-d
(directories), \-e (exported variables), \-f (files), \-g (groups), \-j
(jobs), \-k (reserved words), \-s (services), \-u (users), and \-v
(variables), and \f[CR]\-A \f[R]\f[I]action\f[R] select sources.
.PP
The actions of \-A are alias, arrayvar, binding, builtin, command,
directory, disabled, enabled, export, file, function, group, helptopic,
hostname, job, keyword, running, service, setopt, shopt, signal,
stopped, user, and variable.
\f[CR]\-W \f[R]\f[I]wordlist\f[R] splits the expanded wordlist,
\f[CR]\-G \f[R]\f[I]glob\f[R] adds pathname matches, and
\f[CR]\-X \f[R]\f[I]pattern\f[R] removes the candidates that match
pattern, or keeps only those after a leading !, and an unescaped & in
the pattern stands for the word.
.PP
\f[CR]\-P \f[R]\f[I]prefix\f[R] and \f[CR]\-S \f[R]\f[I]suffix\f[R] add
text to each candidate after matching and filtering.
\f[CR]\-V \f[R]\f[I]array\f[R] stores the candidates in an indexed array
and prints nothing.
\-o, \-F, and \-C are accepted without effect.
No candidate returns 1, and an unknown action returns 2.
.SS \f[CR]complete\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]complete [options] [name ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Register how the arguments of each command complete.
A spec offers the candidates of its actions, its \f[CR]\-G\f[R] glob,
its \f[CR]\-W\f[R] words, and, on an explicit Tab, the COMPREPLY of its
\f[CR]\-F\f[R] function and the output lines of its \f[CR]\-C\f[R]
command, in that order.
The \f[CR]\-C\f[R] command runs as a command substitution with the
command, the word, and the previous word as arguments and COMP_LINE,
COMP_POINT, COMP_KEY, and COMP_TYPE exported.
.PP
A completion function receives the same arguments and the COMP_
variables described under Shell variables.
Trailing spaces are removed from each COMPREPLY entry, the spec\[aq]s
\-X, \-P, and \-S apply, and with the filenames option a slash is
appended to each directory.
The ghost suggestion never runs a function or command.
The actions are those of \f[CR]compgen\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]\-o default\f[R] and \f[CR]\-o bashdefault\f[R] fall back to
filenames and to the shell completion when the spec finds nothing,
\f[CR]\-o filenames\f[R] treats candidates as filenames,
\f[CR]\-o dirnames\f[R] adds matching directories when nothing else
matched, and \f[CR]\-o plusdirs\f[R] adds them always.
A spec quotes inserted candidates only when it generates filenames, as
in Bash.
.PP
\f[CR]\-o fullquote\f[R] quotes every candidate, \f[CR]\-o noquote\f[R]
none, \f[CR]\-o nosort\f[R] keeps the generated order, and
\f[CR]\-o nospace\f[R] adds no space.
An empty result falls through to the next completion source.
An unknown \f[CR]\-o\f[R] option or \f[CR]\-A\f[R] action returns 2 and
registers nothing.
.PP
With \f[CR]progcomp_alias\f[R] on, a command without a spec uses the
spec of the first word of its alias, and an explicit Tab follows a PATH
command to its symlink target.
A command still without a spec uses the \f[CR]\-D\f[R] spec, and when
its function returns 124 completion restarts with the spec it
registered.
.PP
\f[CR]\-E\f[R] serves an explicit Tab on an empty line and
\f[CR]\-I\f[R] the command word.
Their functions receive \f[CR]_EmptycmD_\f[R] and
\f[CR]_InitialWorD_\f[R] as the command.
Of several of \f[CR]\-D\f[R], \f[CR]\-E\f[R], and \f[CR]\-I\f[R], the
first in that order applies.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints the specs in Bash order, and \f[CR]\-r\f[R]
removes the given specs, or all of them.
A missing spec returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]compopt\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]compopt [\-o|+o option] [\-DEI] [name ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[CR]\-o \f[R]\f[I]option\f[R] enables a \f[CR]complete \-o\f[R]
option, and \f[CR]+o \f[R]\f[I]option\f[R] disables it.
If both forms name the same option, it is disabled.
It applies to each given command\[aq]s spec, or to the first of the
\f[CR]\-D\f[R], \f[CR]\-E\f[R], and \f[CR]\-I\f[R] specs, or, inside a
completion function with neither, to the current completion.
.PP
Without \f[CR]\-o\f[R] or \f[CR]+o\f[R], it prints one
\f[CR]compopt\f[R] line per spec.
An unknown option returns 2, and a missing spec, or a missing target
outside a completion function, returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]declare\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]declare [\-aAfFgilnprtux] [name[=value] ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]typeset [\-aAfFgilnprtux] [name[=value] ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Set variables and their attributes.
Inside a function the variables are local unless \f[CR]\-g\f[R] is
given.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R] declares an indexed array, \f[CR]\-A\f[R] an associative
array, \f[CR]\-i\f[R] an integer, whose assignments are evaluated as
arithmetic, \f[CR]\-l\f[R] and \f[CR]\-u\f[R] lowercase and uppercase
conversion, \f[CR]\-r\f[R] a read\-only variable, and \f[CR]\-x\f[R] an
exported one.
.PP
A plus removes an attribute, except that a read\-only attribute stays.
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] is accepted without effect.
\f[CR]\-f\f[R] and \f[CR]\-F\f[R] print functions with or without their
definitions.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints declarations, and without operands \f[CR]\-p\f[R]
or an attribute option lists every variable with those attributes.
Without options or operands, declare prints nothing.
\-a with \-A returns 2, and a missing variable or function, or an
invalid identifier, returns 1.
.PP
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] makes a reference to the variable in its value, which may
carry a subscript.
Expansion, assignment, append, export, and unset act on the target,
following chains, with the target\[aq]s read\-only, restricted, integer,
and case rules.
${!name} gives the target, \f[CR]unset \-n\f[R] and \f[CR]+n\f[R] remove
the reference, and [[ \-R name ]] is true for a reference with a value.
.PP
A reference without a value binds at its first assignment.
A self\-reference is an error at top level.
A circular chain expands as unset without a message, and an assignment
through it is an error.
A read\-only variable, PATH, IFS, and a dynamic variable other than
RANDOM and SECONDS cannot become a reference.
.SS \f[CR]dirs\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]dirs [\-clpv] [+N|\-N]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the current directory and the saved directory stack on one line,
with HOME shown as \[ti].
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] clears the stack, \f[CR]\-l\f[R] prints full paths,
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints one entry per line, and \f[CR]\-v\f[R] numbers
them from 0.
\f[CR]+\f[R]\f[I]N\f[R] prints the Nth entry from the top and
\f[CR]\-\f[R]\f[I]N\f[R] the Nth from the bottom.
An entry past the end returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]disown\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]disown [\-ahr] [job ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Remove each job, by default the current job, from the job table.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-all\f[R] removes every job and \f[CR]\-r\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-running\f[R] every running job.
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-nohup\f[R] keeps the job in the table.
A missing job returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]echo\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]echo [\-neE] [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the arguments separated by spaces and followed by a newline.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] omits the newline, \f[CR]\-e\f[R] interprets backslash
escapes, and \f[CR]\-E\f[R] does not.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood interprets escapes by default.
The escapes are those of $\[aq]...\[aq], except that an octal escape is
\[rs]0\f[I]nnn\f[R] in the Bash moods, and \[rs]c ends the output.
.PP
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\f[R] moods the options may be
combined, as in \-ne, and any other argument that begins with a dash is
printed.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, as in dash, only \-n is an option, escapes
are interpreted by default, \[rs]e and \[rs]x are not escapes, and
\[rs]u is.
\f[CR]xpg_echo\f[R] makes the Bash moods interpret escapes by default.
On Windows, echo in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood leaves backslashes literal
unless \f[CR]\-e\f[R] is given.
A write error returns 1, or 2 in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.SS \f[CR]enable\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]enable [\-adnps] [\-f file] [name ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Enable each builtin or bundled utility, or disable it with
\f[CR]\-n\f[R].
A utility alias such as \f[CR]egrep\f[R] is rejected and follows its
utility.
Each builtin word is separate, so disabling \f[CR]test\f[R] leaves
\f[CR][\f[R] enabled.
Lookup skips a disabled builtin and searches PATH and the bundled
utilities, and a disabled bundled utility never resolves as a command,
though the \f[CR]koshkit\f[R] builtin still runs it.
A disabled special builtin loses its special properties.
A subshell inherits the state, and its changes do not reach the parent.
.PP
Without an operand, and with \f[CR]\-p\f[R], enable lists the enabled
builtins and reachable utilities as \f[CR]enable\f[R] commands,
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] the disabled ones, and \f[CR]\-a\f[R] both.
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] limits a listing to special builtins.
A builtin that the mood hides, such as \f[CR]let\f[R] in the
\f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, is neither listed nor accepted.
Koshka has no loadable builtins, so \f[CR]\-f\f[R] with an operand and
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] return 1, and so does a name that is not a builtin or
utility.
.SS \f[CR]eval\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]eval [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Join the arguments with spaces and run the result as shell input in the
current shell.
The status is that of the last command, or 0 for empty input.
A syntax error in the input is reported with an eval: location and
returns 2.
.SS \f[CR]exec\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]exec [\-cl] [\-a name] [command [argument ...]]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Replace the shell with command.
Without a command, apply the redirections to the shell itself.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R] passes name as argument zero, \f[CR]\-l\f[R] puts a dash
before argument zero, and \f[CR]\-c\f[R] runs the command with an empty
environment.
A command that is not found or cannot be run ends a non\-interactive
shell with status 127 or 126, in every mood, and returns that status in
an interactive shell.
A restricted shell refuses exec.
.SS \f[CR]exit\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]exit [n]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run the EXIT trap and exit with status n modulo 256, or with the status
of the last command.
A non\-numeric operand prints an error and returns 2 without exiting,
except in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood, where the shell exits with status 2.
More than one operand exits with status 1.
.SS \f[CR]export\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]export [\-fnp] [name[=value] ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Mark each variable for the environment of later commands, after
assigning the value when one is given.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] removes the mark, \f[CR]\-f\f[R] marks functions, and
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] or no operand prints the exported variables.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R] moods print them as export
commands and the Bash moods as declare \-x commands.
An invalid identifier and a read\-only variable return 1.
.SS \f[CR]false\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]false [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Return 1.
.SS \f[CR]fc\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]fc [\-e editor] [\-lnr] [first [last]]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]fc \-s [old=new ...] [command]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Edit and run history events.
A number selects an event, a negative number is relative to the current
position, and text selects the newest event with that prefix.
The default is the previous event.
\f[CR]\-l\f[R] lists events, the previous sixteen by default,
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] omits numbers, and \f[CR]\-r\f[R] reverses the order.
.PP
The editor comes from \f[CR]\-e\f[R], FCEDIT, or EDITOR, and defaults to
vi, or ed in the POSIX moods.
An editor of \f[CR]\-\f[R] runs the event unedited.
\f[CR]fc \-s\f[R] applies each literal replacement to one event, prints
it to standard error, runs it, and returns its status.
A text that matches no event returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]fg\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]fg [job]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Resume a job in the foreground, by default the current job, and return
its status.
A missing job returns 1, or 2 in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.SS \f[CR]getopts\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]getopts optstring name [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Parse the options in the arguments, or in the positional parameters.
Each call stores the next option letter in name and its argument in
OPTARG, and OPTIND holds the index of the next argument.
A letter followed by a colon in optstring takes an argument.
An unknown option sets name to ?
and prints a diagnostic.
.PP
OPTERR=0 silences the diagnostics.
A leading colon in optstring also silences them, and then an unknown
option stores the letter in OPTARG and a missing argument sets name to
:.
The status is 0 while options remain and 1 at their end.
.SS \f[CR]hash\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]hash [\-rRt] [\-p path] [name ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Find each name in PATH and remember its location.
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] forgets every location, and \f[CR]\-R\f[R] also searches
PATH again.
\f[CR]\-p \f[R]\f[I]path\f[R] records path for each name.
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] prints the remembered location of each name.
A name that cannot be found returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]help\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]help [\-dms] [name ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the help of each builtin, as its \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] flag does.
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] prints the short description, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] the
synopsis, and \f[CR]\-m\f[R] a manual page.
Without a name, list the builtins.
A name that is not a builtin returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]history\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]history [\-cnrwaps] [\-d selector] [operand]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]history \-S|\-\-sync\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Without a maintenance option, list the events, limited to the newest N
with a numeric operand.
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] loads the history file, or the file operand, into the
list, and \f[CR]\-n\f[R] loads only events not yet loaded.
\f[CR]\-w\f[R] writes the list to the history file or replaces the file
operand.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R] appends the events of the private list that the file
lacks.
.PP
\f[CR]\-S\f[R] replaces the private list with a snapshot of the shared
file.
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] clears the private list and leaves the file intact.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints its operands without storing them, and
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] stores them as one event.
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] deletes an event number, a negative offset from the
newest event, or an inclusive range, and an interactive shell also
removes it from the file.
An event out of range returns 1.
A restricted shell refuses file operands.
.SS \f[CR]jobs\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]jobs [\-lnprs] [job ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
List the jobs, as described under Job control.
\f[CR]\-l\f[R] adds the process id, \f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints only process
ids, \f[CR]\-r\f[R] and \f[CR]\-s\f[R] limit the list to running or
stopped jobs, and \f[CR]\-n\f[R] to jobs that changed since the last
report.
A missing job returns 1, or 2 in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.SS \f[CR]kill\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]kill [\-s signal | \-n num | \-signal] pid|job ...\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]kill \-l [signal|status ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Send a signal, by default SIGTERM, to each process or job.
A signal is a number or a name with or without SIG.
\f[CR]\-l\f[R] lists the signals, or translates each number, status
above 128, or name.
No operand returns 2, and an invalid signal or a process that cannot be
signaled returns 1.
In the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood an invalid signal returns 2.
.SS \f[CR]let\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]let expression ...\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate each expression, as described under Arithmetic evaluation.
The status is 0 when the last value is nonzero and 1 when it is zero.
No expression returns 1.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood has no let.
.SS \f[CR]local\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]local [\-aAilnprux] [name[=value] ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Make variables local to the running function, with the attributes of the
same options of \f[CR]declare\f[R].
\f[CR]local \-n\f[R] passes a variable of the caller by reference, and a
\f[CR]\-\f[R] operand saves the \f[CR]set\f[R] options until the
function returns.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] lists the local variables.
Outside a function local returns 1, and in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood it
ends a non\-interactive shell with status 2.
.SS \f[CR]logout\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]logout [n]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
In a login shell, run the EXIT trap and exit with the optional status.
In another shell, report an error and return 1.
.SS \f[CR]mapfile\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]mapfile [options] [array]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]readarray [options] [array]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Store the lines of standard input in an indexed array, MAPFILE by
default.
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] strips delimiters, \f[CR]\-n\f[R] limits the count, with
0 for every line, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] skips leading lines, \f[CR]\-O\f[R]
sets the first index, \f[CR]\-d\f[R] uses the first byte of its value as
delimiter, NUL when empty, \f[CR]\-u\f[R] reads a descriptor, and
\f[CR]\-C\f[R] runs a callback with the index and the line every
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] lines, 5000 by default.
.SS \f[CR]newgrp\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]newgrp [group]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Replace the shell with the newgrp program found in PATH, which starts a
new shell with the group.
A missing program returns 127, and one that cannot run returns 126.
A restricted shell refuses newgrp.
.SS \f[CR]popd\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]popd [\-n] [+N|\-N]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Remove the top entry, or entry N, from the directory stack and change to
the new top.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] removes the entry below the current directory without
changing directory.
An empty stack returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]printf\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]printf [\-v variable] format [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the arguments under the control of format, which is reused while
arguments remain.
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] assigns the result to a variable or array element.
The format accepts the C conversions d, i, o, u, x, X, e, E, f, F, g, G,
c, and s with flags, width, and precision, * for a width or precision
from an argument, %b for an argument with backslash escapes, %q for an
argument quoted for reuse, and %(\f[I]format\f[R])T for a time in
seconds, formatted by strftime, with \-1 for the current time.
.PP
An argument that is not a valid number prints 0 and makes the status 1.
A missing format returns 2.
A field width or precision over 16777216 is an error, and that
conversion prints nothing.
.PP
The numeric locale is the first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_NUMERIC,
and LANG at each call.
Floating operands use its decimal point, and the \f[CR]\[aq]\f[R] flag
groups digits with its thousands separator.
The C and POSIX locales and every Windows locale use a dot and no
grouping.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood rejects \-v and prints %(\f[I]format\f[R])T as
written.
.SS \f[CR]pushd\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]pushd [\-n] [dir|+N|\-N]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Push dir onto the directory stack and change to it.
Without an operand, swap the two top entries, and with
\f[CR]+\f[R]\f[I]N\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\f[R]\f[I]N\f[R] rotate the stack so
that entry N is on top.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] adds dir below the current directory without changing
directory.
A missing directory or an empty stack returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]pwd\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]pwd [\-LPW]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the working directory.
\f[CR]\-L\f[R] prints the logical path, the default unless the
\f[CR]physical\f[R] option is on, and \f[CR]\-P\f[R] the path with
symbolic links resolved.
\f[CR]\-W\f[R] prints the physical path in Windows form with forward
slashes, as MSYS does, and acts as \f[CR]\-P\f[R] elsewhere.
.SS \f[CR]read\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]read [\-ers] [\-a array] [\-d delim] [\-n|\-N count] [\-p prompt] [\-t timeout] [\-u fd] [\-q] [name ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Read a line from standard input and split it into fields at IFS.
Each name receives one field and the last name receives the rest of the
line.
Without a name the line goes to REPLY.
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] keeps backslashes, and otherwise a trailing backslash
continues the line.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R] splits into an indexed array, \f[CR]\-d\f[R] sets the
delimiter (empty for NUL), \f[CR]\-n\f[R] sets the maximum byte count,
\f[CR]\-N\f[R] reads exactly that many bytes unsplit, ignoring the
delimiter, \f[CR]\-t\f[R] sets a timeout in seconds, which may be
fractional, \f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints a prompt at a terminal, \f[CR]\-s\f[R]
disables echo, \f[CR]\-u\f[R] reads a descriptor, and \f[CR]\-q\f[R]
reads one byte and returns 0 for y or Y.
.PP
\f[CR]\-e\f[R] is accepted without effect.
The status is 1 at the end of input, even when a partial line was
stored, and 142 after a timeout.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood requires a name and rejects every option except
\-r and \-p.
.SS \f[CR]readonly\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]readonly [\-aAfp] [name[=value] ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Mark variables read\-only, after assigning the value when one is given.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] or no operand lists them reusably, \f[CR]\-a\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-A\f[R] treat operands as arrays, and \f[CR]\-f\f[R] marks
functions read\-only, or lists them with their bodies when given alone.
Assigning a read\-only variable is an error, which ends a
non\-interactive shell in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.SS \f[CR]return\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]return [n]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Leave the running function or sourced file with status n modulo 256, or
with the status of the last command.
Outside a function or sourced file return reports an error and returns
2.
A non\-numeric operand returns 2.
.SS \f[CR]set\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]set [\-+abefhkmnprtuvxBCEHPTIARNSGW] [\-+o option] [\-M mood] [\-L moods] [\-\-] [argument ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Change options and positional parameters.
A minus enables an option and a plus disables it.
Arguments after the options, or after \-\-, replace the positional
parameters, and \- also ends the options.
Without arguments, set prints the shell variables as sorted assignments.
\f[CR]set \-o\f[R] \f[I]option\f[R] accepts only Bash spellings.
.PP
Without an option, \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] prints the states and
\f[CR]set +o\f[R] prints reusable commands.
A long option such as \f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R] is rejected with a pointer to
\f[CR]koshconf\f[R].
.PP
The letters are \-a (allexport), \-b (notify, which reports a finished
job at once while the editor waits), \-e (errexit), \-f (noglob), \-h
(hashall, accepted without effect), \-k (keyword, which places
assignment arguments in the command environment), \-m (monitor, job
control), \-n (noexec), \-p (privileged), \-t (onecmd, which stops after
one top\-level command), \-u (nounset), \-v (verbose), \-x (xtrace), \-B
(braceexpand), \-C (noclobber), \-E (errtrace), \-H (histexpand), \-P
(physical), and \-T (functrace).
Options without a letter are emacs, vi, history, ignoreeof,
interactive\-comments, nolog (accepted without effect), pipefail, and
posix, which selects the \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] mood, while
\f[CR]set +o posix\f[R] selects \f[CR]bash\f[R] from the
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] or \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
The options are described under shell settings.
.PP
The Koshka forms \f[CR]set \-M \f[R]\f[I]MOOD\f[R] and
\f[CR]set \-L \f[R]\f[I]MOOD,...\f[R] select the mood and source the
startup files of other moods as the command\-line options do.
Without a value they print the mood or the initialized moods.
\f[CR]\-I\f[R], \f[CR]\-A\f[R], \f[CR]\-R\f[R], \f[CR]\-N\f[R],
\f[CR]\-S\f[R], and \f[CR]\-G\f[R] set interpreter.mimic_shebang,
debug.print_syntax_tree, debug.print_lexed_word_escapes,
debug.report_every_exit_code, debug.print_evaluation_statistics, and
debug.print_memory_report_at_exit, and \f[CR]\-W\f[R], \f[CR]\-WW\f[R],
and \f[CR]\-WWW\f[R] set optimizer.warning_level.
.PP
Every other Koshka setting belongs to \f[CR]koshconf\f[R].
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] turns restricted mode on, and \f[CR]+r\f[R] fails with
status 1.
An unknown option returns 1 in the Bash moods, ends a non\-interactive
shell with status 1 in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, and with status 2 in
the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, a value other than the one held for
nounset, pipefail, failglob, or nullglob is an error.
.SS \f[CR]shift\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]shift [n]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Discard the first n positional parameters, by default 1.
A count above $# or below 0 returns 1, and in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood it
ends a non\-interactive shell with status 2.
.SS \f[CR]shopt\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]shopt [\-pqsuo] [option ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Show or change the \f[CR]shopt\f[R] options, which are described under
shell settings.
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] enables and \f[CR]\-u\f[R] disables each option, or
without one lists the options that are on or off.
Without \-s or \-u, shopt prints the state of each option, or of all of
them, and returns 1 when one is off.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] prints reusable shopt commands, \f[CR]\-o\f[R] selects
the \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] options, and \f[CR]\-q\f[R] prints nothing.
An unknown option returns 1 after the valid ones are applied.
.SS \f[CR]suspend\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]suspend [\-f]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Stop the shell until it receives SIGCONT.
A login shell refuses without \f[CR]\-f\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]time\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]time [\-p] [\-R] pipeline\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A reserved word, described under Pipelines.
.SS \f[CR]times\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]times\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the user and system times of the shell, then of its children.
.SS \f[CR]trap\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]trap [\-lp] [[action] condition ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Set action to run when the shell receives each signal or meets each
condition.
The conditions are the signals by number or by name with or without SIG,
EXIT or 0 at exit, ERR after a failing command where errexit would act,
DEBUG before each simple command, and RETURN at the end of a function or
sourced file.
.PP
An empty action ignores the signal, and an action of \- resets the
default.
Without arguments, and with \f[CR]\-p\f[R], trap prints the actions as
reusable trap commands, and \f[CR]\-l\f[R] lists the signals.
An invalid signal returns 1.
A trap action runs with the status of the command before it, and it does
not change that status.
The \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood rejects ERR, RETURN, \-l, and \-p.
.SS \f[CR]type\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]type [\-aPptV] name ...\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Describe how each name is interpreted as a command.
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] prints one of alias, keyword, function, builtin, or file,
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] and \f[CR]\-P\f[R] print the path of a program,
\f[CR]\-a\f[R] prints every interpretation, and \f[CR]\-V\f[R] prints
everything the shell holds about each word, such as a function\[aq]s
definition site and body or the description of a builtin or utility.
.PP
The Bash moods report a function as \[dq]is a function\[dq] followed by
its body, and the other moods as \[dq]is a shell function\[dq].
A name that is not found returns 1, and 127 in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.SS \f[CR]ulimit\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]ulimit [\-HSa] [\-cdflmnpstuvw] [limit]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Show or set a resource limit, by default the file size limit \-f.
\f[CR]\-H\f[R] and \f[CR]\-S\f[R] select the hard or the soft limit, and
\f[CR]\-a\f[R] prints every limit.
The resources are \-c (core size), \-d (data size), \-f (file size), \-l
(locked memory), \-m (resident set), \-n (open files), \-p (pipe size),
\-s (stack size), \-t (CPU seconds), \-u (processes), \-v (virtual
memory), and \-w (file locks).
A limit is a number or unlimited.
An invalid limit, or one above the hard limit, returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]umask\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]umask [\-pS] [mask]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Show or set the file creation mask.
A mask is octal or symbolic, as in u=rwx,g=rx,o=.
\f[CR]\-S\f[R] prints the mask symbolically, and \f[CR]\-p\f[R] as a
reusable command.
An invalid mask returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]unalias\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]unalias [\-a] name ...\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Remove each alias, or every alias with \f[CR]\-a\f[R].
An unknown alias returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]unset\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]unset [\-fnv] name ...\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Remove each variable or array element, or each function with
\f[CR]\-f\f[R].
Without an option a variable is removed, or a function when no such
variable exists.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] removes a name reference itself.
A read\-only variable returns 1, and an unset name succeeds.
.SS \f[CR]wait\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]wait [\-fn] [\-p variable] [pid|job ...]\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Wait for each process or job and return the status of the last one, or
wait for every child and return 0.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] waits for the next job to finish among the operands, or
among all jobs, and returns 127 when none is left.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] \f[I]variable\f[R] stores the process whose status is
returned, or unsets the variable.
.PP
\f[CR]\-f\f[R] waits until each job terminates.
A stopped job returns 128 plus the stop signal, and \f[CR]wait \-n\f[R]
skips it.
A process that is not a child of the shell, or a job that does not
exist, returns 127 with a diagnostic, and a missing job number returns 2
in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
.SS Standard utilities
These utilities run through \f[CR]koshkit\f[R] or their utility names,
as described under Bundled utilities.
.SS \f[CR]basename\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]basename\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the final path component, minus an optional suffix.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-multiple\f[R] treats every operand as a path,
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-suffix\f[R] removes a suffix from each and
implies \f[CR]\-a\f[R], and \f[CR]\-z\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-zero\f[R] ends
entries with NUL.
.SS \f[CR]bc\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]bc\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate expressions, assignments, functions, arrays, control flow,
input and output bases, and scale, from the file operands and then
standard input.
\f[CR]\-l\f[R] adds the functions s (sine), c (cosine), a (arctangent),
l (natural logarithm), and e (exponential) and sets scale to 20, and
\f[CR]\-q\f[R] suppresses the banner.
Division by zero stops the input with status 1.
.SS \f[CR]cal\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]cal\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print a Gregorian month or year with weeks starting on Sunday.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-today\f[R] starts weeks on Monday and prints
the date in words below.
.SS \f[CR]cat\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]cat\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Concatenate files or standard input.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] numbers lines across files, \f[CR]\-b\f[R] numbers
nonempty lines, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] squeezes empty runs, \f[CR]\-v\f[R] shows
control bytes in \[ha] notation and high bytes with M\-, \f[CR]\-E\f[R]
marks line ends with $ and a CR before a newline as \[ha]M,
\f[CR]\-T\f[R] shows tabs as \[ha]I, and \f[CR]\-e\f[R], \f[CR]\-t\f[R],
and \f[CR]\-A\f[R] act as \f[CR]\-vE\f[R], \f[CR]\-vT\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-vET\f[R].
\f[CR]\-u\f[R] is accepted.
\f[CR]\-\-syntax\-highlighting\f[R] colors shell source on a terminal.
.SS \f[CR]chgrp\f[R] and \f[CR]chown\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]chgrp, chown\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Change the group, or the owner and optional group, of each file.
\f[CR]\-R\f[R] recurses, \f[CR]\-H\f[R], \f[CR]\-L\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-P\f[R] select which links are followed, \f[CR]\-h\f[R] changes a
link itself, and \f[CR]\-x\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-one\-file\-system\f[R] keeps
recursion on one file system.
.SS \f[CR]chmod\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]chmod\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Apply symbolic or octal modes.
\f[CR]\-R\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-recursive\f[R] recurses without following
links, and \f[CR]\-\-one\-file\-system\f[R] skips other file systems.
.SS \f[CR]cksum\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]cksum\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the POSIX CRC checksum and byte count of each file or standard
input, followed by the file.
.SS \f[CR]cmp\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]cmp\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Compare two files byte by byte.
A difference prints the first differing byte and line and returns 1,
equal files return 0, and an unreadable file returns 2.
\f[CR]\-l\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R] lists every differing byte with
both values in octal, and \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-silent\f[R] prints
nothing.
A file of \- is standard input.
.SS \f[CR]comm\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]comm\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Merge two sorted files into columns of lines only in the first, only in
the second, and in both; \f[CR]\-1\f[R], \f[CR]\-2\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-3\f[R] suppress them.
A line out of order after an unpaired line is reported once per file
with status 1; \f[CR]\-\-check\-order\f[R] stops at the first line out
of order, and \f[CR]\-\-nocheck\-order\f[R] skips the check, the later
one winning.
\f[CR]\-\-output\-delimiter\f[R] sets the column separator (empty for
NUL), \f[CR]\-\-total\f[R] appends column counts, and \f[CR]\-z\f[R]
uses NUL line ends.
.SS \f[CR]cp\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]cp\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Copy files and trees with \f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-R\f[R],
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-p\f[R], and \f[CR]\-v\f[R].
A recursive copy keeps links as links; \f[CR]\-L\f[R] follows every
link, \f[CR]\-H\f[R] only links on the command line, and \f[CR]\-P\f[R]
none.
A link leading back into a directory being copied is reported as cyclic
and skipped with status 1.
\f[CR]\-x\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-one\-file\-system\f[R] copies a directory on
another file system as an empty directory.
.SS \f[CR]csplit\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]csplit\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Split a file into pieces at each pattern, which is a line number or a
/\f[I]regex\f[R]/, and print the byte count of each piece.
The pieces are xx00, xx01, and so on.
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-prefix\f[R] replaces xx, \f[CR]\-n\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-digits\f[R] sets the number of digits, \f[CR]\-s\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-silent\f[R] suppresses the counts, and \f[CR]\-k\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-keep\-files\f[R] keeps the pieces after an error.
A line number past the input or a regex that does not match returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]cut\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]cut\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Select bytes, characters, or fields with \f[CR]\-b\f[R], \f[CR]\-c\f[R],
or \f[CR]\-f\f[R].
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] sets the delimiter, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] skips lines without
one, \f[CR]\-\-complement\f[R] inverts the selection,
\f[CR]\-\-output\-delimiter=\f[R]\f[I]str\f[R] joins selections with
str, and \f[CR]\-z\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-zero\-terminated\f[R] uses NUL line
ends.
With \f[CR]\-b\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R] leaves out a multibyte character
that the byte range splits.
An invalid list or a delimiter longer than one byte returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]date\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]date\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the date and time, or format them with a +\f[I]format\f[R] operand
of strftime conversions.
\f[CR]\-u\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-utc\f[R] uses UTC.
An operand that would set the clock is refused.
.SS \f[CR]df\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]df\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Report mounted file systems or those of the operands in a right\-aligned
table.
\f[CR]\-k\f[R] uses 1024\-byte blocks, \f[CR]\-P\f[R] the POSIX header,
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-human\-readable\f[R] powers of 1024, and
\f[CR]\-H\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-si\f[R] powers of 1000.
Unreadable file systems are reported before the table.
.SS \f[CR]diff\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]diff\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Compare two files line by line.
\f[CR]\-u\f[R] selects unified output, \f[CR]\-w\f[R] ignores
whitespace, \f[CR]\-a\f[R] forces text, and up to two \f[CR]\-L\f[R]
options set the unified labels.
\f[CR]\-\-color\f[R][=\f[I]WHEN\f[R]] takes auto (the default), always,
or never.
The status is 0 for equal files, 1 for different files, and 2 for an
error.
.SS \f[CR]dirname\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]dirname\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the directory part of each path.
\f[CR]\-z\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-zero\f[R] ends entries with NUL.
.SS \f[CR]du\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]du\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print every file and directory below each path, or the current
directory, in descending size order without following links.
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] prints totals only, \f[CR]\-h\f[R] human\-readable sizes,
\f[CR]\-x\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-one\-file\-system\f[R] skips other file
systems, \f[CR]\-\-tree\f[R] prints a tree, and \f[CR]\-T\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-top\-largest\f[R] a tree of the largest entries and their
ancestors.
Permission errors are warnings, other read failures are errors, and
either makes the status 1.
.SS \f[CR]env\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]env\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run a command with temporary assignments, or print the environment.
\f[CR]\-i\f[R] starts empty, and each \f[CR]\-u\f[R] removes a variable.
The command is found through its PATH, or /usr/bin:/bin without one.
.SS \f[CR]expand\f[R] and \f[CR]unexpand\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]expand, unexpand\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Convert tabs to spaces, or blanks to tabs.
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] takes ascending tab stops separated by commas or blanks,
or one interval; a last value /N adds a stop at each multiple of N, and
+N one every N columns, after the last explicit stop.
The default is every eight columns.
\f[CR]unexpand \-a\f[R] also converts blanks after the first nonblank,
and \f[CR]\-t\f[R] implies it.
.SS \f[CR]expr\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]expr\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate an expression whose operators and operands are separate
arguments and print the result.
The operators are |, &, =, !=, <, <=, >, >=, +, \-, *, /, %, and :,
which matches a basic regular expression anchored at the start and
prints the first group or the match length.
The status is 0 for a result that is neither 0 nor empty, 1 otherwise,
and 2 for an invalid expression.
Integer overflow and division by zero are errors.
.SS \f[CR]file\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]file\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Classify file kinds and contents.
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-no\-dereference\f[R] classifies a link and
\f[CR]\-L\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dereference\f[R] its target, \f[CR]\-i\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-regular\-only\f[R] skips content tests, \f[CR]\-m\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-magic\-file\f[R] adds a magic database, and \f[CR]\-M\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-magic\-only\f[R] uses only the given ones.
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-default\-tests\f[R] selects the default tests.
.PP
The kinds include text, data, directory, the special files, ELF
executable, common image formats, script text executable, and symbolic
link to a target.
A file that cannot be opened returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]find\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]find\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Walk sorted trees with \f[CR]\-name\f[R], \f[CR]\-iname\f[R],
\f[CR]\-type\f[R], \f[CR]\-maxdepth\f[R], \f[CR]\-mindepth\f[R],
\f[CR]\-print\f[R], and \f[CR]\-print0\f[R].
\f[CR]\-xdev\f[R] and \f[CR]\-mount\f[R] stay on one file system.
\f[CR]\-exec\f[R] \f[I]command\f[R] ...
\f[I];\f[R] runs once per path with {} replaced, and \f[CR]\-exec\f[R]
\f[I]command\f[R] {} \f[I]+\f[R] runs on batches of paths.
.SS \f[CR]flock\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]flock\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run a command while holding a lock on a file or directory; the command
inherits the descriptor.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-nonblock\f[R] fails with status 1 instead of
waiting, and \f[CR]\-\-transaction\-held\-lock\f[R] keeps the lock after
the launching process exits.
.SS \f[CR]fold\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]fold\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Wrap the lines of each file or standard input at 80 columns, or at the
width of \f[CR]\-w\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-width\f[R].
A tab advances to the next multiple of eight.
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-spaces\f[R] breaks after the last blank when
one fits, and \f[CR]\-b\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-bytes\f[R] counts bytes.
.SS \f[CR]fuser\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]fuser\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the pids that use each file, with \f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-f\f[R],
and \f[CR]\-u\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]getconf\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]getconf\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print a system or string variable, or a path variable for a path; an
unsupported one prints undefined.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-all\f[R] prints every variable, and
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-specification\f[R] selects a POSIX
environment.
.SS \f[CR]grep\f[R], \f[CR]egrep\f[R], and \f[CR]fgrep\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]grep, egrep, fgrep\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print lines matching basic regular expressions, or extended ones with
\f[CR]\-E\f[R] (egrep), or fixed strings with \f[CR]\-F\f[R] (fgrep);
the two conflict with status 2.
\f[CR]\-e\f[R] adds a pattern and \f[CR]\-f\f[R] the lines of a file,
and with either every operand is a file; a newline separates patterns.
\f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-x\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R],
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], and \f[CR]\-r\f[R] ignore case, invert, match whole
lines, number lines, omit file prefixes, and recurse, and
\f[CR]\-\-one\-file\-system\f[R] keeps \f[CR]\-r\f[R] on one file
system.
.PP
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] counts, \f[CR]\-l\f[R] lists matching files and overrides
\f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-q\f[R] exits 0 at the first match, and
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] hides unreadable\-file messages.
In a file with a NUL byte, matches before the line of the first NUL
print, the next one shows a single warning, and later ones print
nothing, unless \f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-text\f[R] treats it as text.
.PP
In a UTF\-8 locale a dot and a bracket expression match one character,
and one byte otherwise.
The status is 0 when a line is selected, 1 when none is, and 2 on error.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-color\f[R][=\f[I]WHEN\f[R]] takes auto (the default), always,
or never.
GREP_COLORS holds colon\-separated GNU capabilities such as ms=01;31:
ms, mt, sl, cx, fn, ln, se, and rv apply, unknown ones are ignored, and
an empty value turns that color off.
GREP_COLOR sets the match color below GREP_COLORS.
.SS \f[CR]head\f[R] and \f[CR]tail\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]head, tail\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the first or last ten lines.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] sets a line count and \f[CR]\-c\f[R] a byte count, with
an optional suffix: b for 512, k, K, m, M, G, T, P, E, Z, Y, R, or Q for
powers of 1024, and those letters followed by B for powers of 1000 or by
iB, as in 1kB and 1KiB.
A first argument such as \-5 means \f[CR]\-n 5\f[R].
.PP
For head, a leading dash prints all but the last N; for tail, a leading
plus starts at line or byte N. Headers appear for several files;
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-silent\f[R] suppress
them and \f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R] forces them, the last
winning.
\f[CR]\-z\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-zero\-terminated\f[R] uses NUL line ends.
.PP
\f[CR]tail \-f\f[R] keeps printing what is appended to each regular
file, and \f[CR]\-F\f[R] follows by name, reopening a replaced, removed,
or new file, and implies \f[CR]\-\-retry\f[R].
\f[CR]\-\-follow=\f[R]\f[I]descriptor|name\f[R] selects the mode,
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-sleep\-interval\f[R] the longest wait between
checks (1 second), and \f[CR]\-\-pid=\f[R]\f[I]pid\f[R] ends following
after that process exits.
An interrupted follow exits with status 130.
.SS \f[CR]id\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]id\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the user and group identities of the current process or of a user.
\f[CR]\-u\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-user\f[R], \f[CR]\-g\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-group\f[R], and \f[CR]\-G\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-groups\f[R] print
only the user, the group, or every group, \f[CR]\-n\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-name\f[R] prints names, and \f[CR]\-r\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-real\f[R] uses the real identities.
An unknown user returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]killall\f[R] and \f[CR]pkill\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]killall, pkill\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[CR]killall\f[R] signals processes by exact name, and \f[CR]pkill\f[R]
those whose short command contains the pattern.
The signal comes from \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-signal\f[R] in killall,
and from \f[CR]\-\-signal\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\f[R]\f[I]SIGNAL\f[R] in
pkill; \f[CR]\-l\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-list\f[R] lists signals.
pkill \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-session\f[R] takes comma\-separated
session ids, 0 for its own, and then the pattern is optional; Windows
has no sessions, so 0 is rejected and nothing matches another id.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-inverse\f[R] signals what the pattern and sessions do not
select.
Process 0, the shell, and the utility itself are never signaled by
killall.
.SS \f[CR]less\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]less\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Show files or standard input full screen on the alternate screen.
The keys come from the terminal.
Space, f, Ctrl\-F, Ctrl\-V, and Page Down move forward a page, b,
Ctrl\-B, and Page Up back a page, d and Ctrl\-D forward half a page, u
and Ctrl\-U back half a page, j, e, Enter, Down, Ctrl\-E, and Ctrl\-N
one row down, and k, y, Up, Ctrl\-P, and Ctrl\-Y one row up.
.PP
The mouse wheel scrolls three rows.
g, <, and Home go to the top, G, >, and End to the bottom, and r and
Ctrl\-L redraw.
\f[CR]/\f[R]\f[I]pattern\f[R] and \f[CR]?\f[R]\f[I]pattern\f[R] search
forward and backward with an extended regular expression, and n and N
repeat the search.
An empty pattern repeats the previous one, Escape cancels the prompt,
and an invalid pattern is reported.
q and Q quit, and an interrupt quits with status 130.
.PP
The status row shows the file, the visible lines and their total, the
position, and (END) at the end.
Input loads as needed and is kept in memory up to 128 MiB, counting the
line records.
Input beyond that is not shown, and the status row adds (truncated) at
the end.
\f[CR]\-N\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-LINE\-NUMBERS\f[R] numbers the lines,
\f[CR]\-S\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-chop\-long\-lines\f[R] cuts long lines instead
of wrapping them, and \f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R]
ignores case in searches.
Several files follow headers of colons.
When output is not a terminal, the input is copied unchanged.
.SS \f[CR]link\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]link\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Create a hard link file2 to file1.
An existing file2 returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]ln\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]ln\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Create hard links, or symbolic links with \f[CR]\-s\f[R].
\f[CR]\-f\f[R] removes an existing destination, \f[CR]\-L\f[R] follows a
link source, and \f[CR]\-P\f[R] links the link itself.
.SS \f[CR]locale\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]locale\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print LANG and the LC_ variables as the environment sets them.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-all\-locales\f[R] lists the available locales
and \f[CR]\-m\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-charmaps\f[R] the character maps.
A keyword operand such as charmap prints its value, \f[CR]\-k\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-keyword\-name\f[R] adds the keyword, and \f[CR]\-c\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-category\-name\f[R] adds its category.
An unknown keyword returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]logger\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]logger\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Send a message, the operands or each line of \f[CR]\-f\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-file\f[R], to the system log.
\f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-tag\f[R] sets the tag, \f[CR]\-p\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-priority\f[R] the facility and severity, as in user.notice,
\f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-id\f[R] adds the process id, and
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-stderr\f[R] also writes the message to
standard error.
An invalid priority returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]logname\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]logname\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the login user, which can differ from the effective user that
\f[CR]whoami\f[R] prints.
.SS \f[CR]ls\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]ls\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
List sorted entries with \f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-A\f[R], \f[CR]\-1\f[R],
\f[CR]\-l\f[R], and \f[CR]\-h\f[R].
\f[CR]\-F\f[R] appends type indicators, \f[CR]\-p\f[R] a slash to
directories, \f[CR]\-d\f[R] lists directories themselves, \f[CR]\-n\f[R]
shows numeric ids, and \f[CR]\-g\f[R] and \f[CR]\-o\f[R] omit the owner
or group.
\f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-S\f[R], and \f[CR]\-r\f[R] sort by time, by
size, and in reverse.
\f[CR]\-R\f[R] recurses, \f[CR]\-\-tree\f[R] prints a tree,
\f[CR]\-L\f[R] limits depth, and \f[CR]\-\-one\-file\-system\f[R] keeps
both on one file system.
\f[CR]\-k\f[R] is accepted.
Without a terminal, ls prints one entry per line, and \f[CR]\-l\f[R]
prints a symbolic link without its target.
.SS \f[CR]make\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]make\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run make rules.
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-file\f[R], \f[CR]\-C\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-directory\f[R], \f[CR]\-B\f[R] (always rebuild),
\f[CR]\-k\f[R] (keep going), and the POSIX modes \f[CR]\-e\f[R],
\f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-S\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-p\f[R],
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-s\f[R], and \f[CR]\-t\f[R] are
supported, and \f[CR]\-j\f[R] takes an optional job count passed through
MAKEFLAGS.
Without \f[CR]\-f\f[R], make reads Makefile or makefile, and
\f[CR]\-f \-\f[R] reads standard input.
Operands of the form \f[I]name\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R] set variables, and
the variables of the environment are also available.
.PP
The functions are abspath, addprefix, addsuffix, and, basename, call,
dir, error, eval, filter, filter\-out, findstring, firstword, flavor,
foreach, if, join, lastword, notdir, or, origin, patsubst, realpath,
shell, sort, strip, subst, suffix, value, warning, wildcard, word,
wordlist, and words.
The directives are include, ifeq, ifneq, ifdef, ifndef, else, endif,
define, override, export, and unexport, and the special targets are
\&.DEFAULT, .IGNORE, .PHONY, .POSIX, .PRECIOUS, .SCCS_GET, .SILENT, and
\&.SUFFIXES.
.PP
The wildcard function and a prerequisite holding *, ?, or [ use shell
pathname expansion, and an unmatched prerequisite pattern stays as
written.
Recipes are printed before they run and use the SHELL of the makefile,
and MAKE expands to koshkit make.
A target without a rule returns 2, and \f[CR]\-q\f[R] returns 1 when a
target is out of date.
.SS \f[CR]man\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]man\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the source of the manual page of each name, from the section
operand or the first section that has one, searching the directories of
MANPATH, by default /usr/share/man and /usr/local/share/man.
\f[CR]\-k\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-keyword\f[R] lists the pages whose first lines
contain the keyword.
A missing page returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]mkdir\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]mkdir\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Create directories, with parents under \f[CR]\-p\f[R].
\f[CR]\-m\f[R] takes an octal mode or a symbolic mode applied to 777.
.SS \f[CR]mkfifo\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]mkfifo\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Create each FIFO, with the mode of \f[CR]\-m\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-mode\f[R]
or 0666 less the creation mask.
.SS \f[CR]mknod\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]mknod\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Create FIFOs and character or block special files from POSIX type
operands or \f[CR]\-\-type\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-fifo\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-character\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-block\f[R].
Device nodes take \f[CR]\-\-major\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-minor\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-m\f[R] sets the mode.
.SS \f[CR]more\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]more\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Show files or standard input a screen at a time with a
\f[CR]\-\-More\-\-\f[R] prompt, with keys from the terminal: space and f
the next page, Enter, j, and Down the next line, d half a page, b, k,
Page Up, and Up further back, g and G the top and the bottom, the
searches of \f[CR]less\f[R], and q, or a forward key at the end, to
quit.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-lines\f[R] sets the lines per screen,
\f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-clear\f[R] clears before each page,
\f[CR]\-e\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-exit\f[R] exits after the last line,
\f[CR]\-p\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-command\f[R] starts at the first line that
matches a pattern, \f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-tag\f[R] at a tag of the
tags file, \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-squeeze\f[R] collapses repeated
blank lines, and \f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R] ignores
case in searches.
.PP
Each of several files follows a header of colons, also when output is
not a terminal.
When output is not a terminal, the input is copied, changed only by
\f[CR]\-p\f[R], \f[CR]\-t\f[R], and \f[CR]\-s\f[R].
A pattern of \f[CR]\-p\f[R] may begin with a slash.
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] reads ./tags, whose lines hold a tag, a file, and a line
separated by tabs, and takes no file operand.
.PP
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] and \f[CR]\-t\f[R] cannot be combined, and more needs a
file when standard input is a terminal.
The status row shows \-\-More\-\- and the position, or (END).
Input is kept in memory up to 128 MiB, counting the line records.
Input beyond that limit is not shown, and the status row adds
(truncated).
\f[CR]\-u\f[R] is accepted.
.SS \f[CR]mv\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]mv\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Rename with \f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-i\f[R], and \f[CR]\-v\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]nice\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]nice\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run a utility with its scheduling priority lowered by 10, or by the
increment of \f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-increment\f[R].
A utility that is not found returns 127.
.SS \f[CR]nl\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]nl\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Number lines within logical pages.
A line of only the section delimiter repeated three, two, or one times
starts a header, body, or footer and prints empty.
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] sets the delimiter, \[rs]: by default, adding a colon to
a single character; an empty delimiter disables sections.
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-b\f[R], and \f[CR]\-f\f[R] set the section
styles (default n, t, n): a every line, t nonempty lines, n none, and
p\f[I]BRE\f[R] lines matching the regular expression.
.PP
Each section restarts at \f[CR]\-v\f[R] unless \f[CR]\-p\f[R] is given,
and \f[CR]\-i\f[R] sets the increment; both may be negative.
\f[CR]\-l\f[R] numbers every Nth empty line of a run under style a.
\f[CR]\-w\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R], and \f[CR]\-s\f[R] set the width (at
most 65536), the ln, rn, or rz format, and the separator.
An overflowing line number stops with status 1.
.SS \f[CR]nohup\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]nohup\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run a utility that ignores SIGHUP.
When standard output is a terminal, the output is appended to nohup.out
in the current directory, or in HOME when that fails, which is created
with mode 0600.
A utility that is not found returns 127, and one that cannot run 126.
.SS \f[CR]nproc\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]nproc\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the available processor count; \f[CR]\-\-all\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-ignore=\f[R]\f[I]COUNT\f[R] are supported.
.SS \f[CR]od\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]od\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Dump bytes in the formats of \f[CR]\-t\f[R]; \f[CR]\-b\f[R],
\f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-o\f[R], \f[CR]\-s\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-x\f[R] select o1, c, u2, o2, d2, and x2.
A line holds 16 bytes, or \f[CR]\-w\f[R][\f[I]N\f[R]] or
\f[CR]\-\-width\f[R][=\f[I]N\f[R]] bytes, up to 65536 or 32 without N. A
width that is not a multiple of the largest unit is replaced by that
unit with a warning.
.PP
The types of \f[CR]\-t\f[R] are c, o, u, d, and x, with an optional
size, and several types print one row each.
\f[CR]\-A\f[R] sets the address radix to d, o, x, or n for none,
\f[CR]\-j\f[R] skips bytes, \f[CR]\-N\f[R] limits the bytes read, and
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] prints repeated lines that are otherwise shown as *.
.SS \f[CR]paste\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]paste\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Join corresponding lines with tabs.
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] sets delimiters used in turn, \f[CR]\-s\f[R] joins each
file into one line, and \f[CR]\-z\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-zero\-terminated\f[R]
uses NUL line ends.
.SS \f[CR]pathchk\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]pathchk\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Check paths against the file system limits.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] uses the POSIX portable limits and characters,
\f[CR]\-P\f[R] rejects an empty path and a component starting with a
hyphen, and \f[CR]\-W\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-windows\f[R] rejects what Windows
cannot hold, as Windows builds always do.
.SS \f[CR]pr\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]pr\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Paginate each file or standard input into 66\-line pages with a header
of the date, the file, and the page number.
+\f[I]first\f[R][:\f[I]last\f[R]] selects pages, \-\f[I]columns\f[R]
prints that many columns, \f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-across\f[R] fills
them across, \f[CR]\-m\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-merge\f[R] prints the files side
by side, and \f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-omit\-header\f[R] omits headers.
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-double\-space\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-number\-lines\f[R], \f[CR]\-o\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-indent\f[R],
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-header\f[R], \f[CR]\-D\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-date\-format\f[R], \f[CR]\-l\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-length\f[R],
\f[CR]\-w\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-width\f[R], and \f[CR]\-s\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-separator\f[R] set spacing, numbering, the indent, the header
text and date format, the page length and width, and the column
separator.
\f[CR]\-F\f[R] and \f[CR]\-f\f[R] separate pages with form feeds, and
\f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-no\-file\-warnings\f[R] suppresses file
warnings.
.SS \f[CR]printenv\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]printenv\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the environment, or the given variables, with status 1 if one is
absent.
.SS \f[CR]ps\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]ps\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
List processes with \f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-u\f[R], and \f[CR]\-x\f[R];
\f[CR]\-w\f[R] is accepted.
.SS \f[CR]readlink\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]readlink\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the target of each symbolic link.
A non\-link is reported with status 1.
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-canonicalize\f[R], \f[CR]\-e\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-canonicalize\-existing\f[R], and \f[CR]\-m\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-canonicalize\-missing\f[R] print the canonical absolute path
with every link resolved, as \f[CR]realpath\f[R] prints it without a
flag, with \f[CR]\-e\f[R], and with \f[CR]\-m\f[R].
The last of the three wins.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-no\-newline\f[R] omits the newline for a
single file.
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R], \f[CR]\-s\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\-silent\f[R] suppress the error messages.
\f[CR]\-z\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-zero\f[R] ends each entry with a NUL byte.
.SS \f[CR]realpath\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]realpath\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the canonical absolute path.
Every component but the last must exist, all with \f[CR]\-e\f[R], and
none with \f[CR]\-m\f[R].
Links resolve as met, or with \f[CR]\-P\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-physical\f[R];
\f[CR]\-L\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-logical\f[R] resolves ..
first, and \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-no\-symlinks\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\-strip\f[R] leave links alone, the last of the three winning.
\f[CR]\-\-relative\-to\f[R] prints paths relative to a directory, and
\f[CR]\-\-relative\-base\f[R] prints paths below a directory relative to
it and others absolute; with both, paths are relative to the first when
it is below the base.
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R] omits errors, and \f[CR]\-z\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-zero\f[R] ends entries with NUL.
.SS \f[CR]renice\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]renice\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Add the increment of the required \f[CR]\-n\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-increment\f[R] to the priority of each process, or of each
process group with \f[CR]\-g\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-pgrp\f[R], or of each
user\[aq]s processes with \f[CR]\-u\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-user\f[R].
\f[CR]\-p\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-pid\f[R] selects process ids, the default.
.SS \f[CR]rm\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]rm\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Remove paths.
\f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-R\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-recursive\f[R] removes
directories and their contents, \f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R]
removes empty directories, \f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-force\f[R] ignores
missing paths and never asks, \f[CR]\-i\f[R] asks before each removal,
\f[CR]\-I\f[R] asks once before removing more than three paths or a
tree, and \f[CR]\-\-interactive=\f[R]\f[I]when\f[R] selects never, once,
or always.
.PP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R] prints each removed path, and
\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] prints what would be removed and removes
nothing.
The operands .
and ..
are refused, and so is every spelling of a root directory or a Windows
drive root unless \f[CR]\-\-no\-preserve\-root\f[R] is given.
\f[CR]\-\-preserve\-root=all\f[R] also refuses an operand on another
device than its parent.
\f[CR]\-x\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-one\-file\-system\f[R] skips directories on
other file systems with status 1.
A missing path returns 1 without \f[CR]\-f\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]rmdir\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]rmdir\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Remove empty directories, and with \f[CR]\-p\f[R] their empty parents.
.SS \f[CR]sed\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]sed\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Apply an editing script, from the first operand or from each
\f[CR]\-e\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-expression\f[R] and \f[CR]\-f\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-file\f[R], to the lines of each file or standard input.
The commands are s with the g and p flags, y, d, p, q, =, and a, i, and
c, whose text follows on the same line or on the next lines after a
backslash, with line, $, and regex addresses and ranges.
.PP
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R] suppresses automatic printing, and
\f[CR]\-E\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-extended\-regexp\f[R] selects extended regular
expressions.
Blocks, the hold space, branches, and in\-place editing are not
supported, and an unsupported command returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]seq\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]seq\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print numbers with a nonzero step.
Integers print exactly, and decimal, exponent, and hexadecimal operands
print with the most fraction digits of the first operand and the step.
\f[CR]\-w\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-equal\-width\f[R] pads with zeros,
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-separator\f[R] sets the separator, and
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R] applies one printf %e, %f, %g, or
%a conversion and excludes \f[CR]\-w\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]sleep\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]sleep\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Pause for the sum of the durations, with optional s, m, h, or d
suffixes.
.SS \f[CR]sort\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]sort\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Sort lines by byte order or by \f[CR]\-k\f[R] keys of the form
\f[I]F[.C][bdfghiMnrV][,F[.C][bdfghiMnrV]]\f[R].
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] sets the separator.
\f[CR]\-b\f[R], \f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-i\f[R],
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], and \f[CR]\-r\f[R] ignore leading blanks, compare only
blanks and alphanumerics, fold case, compare only printable characters,
compare numbers, and reverse.
.PP
\f[CR]\-g\f[R] compares floating numbers, including exponents,
infinities, and NaN, with text first; \f[CR]\-h\f[R] compares K, M, G,
T, P, E, Z, Y, R, and Q suffixes; \f[CR]\-M\f[R] compares month
abbreviations with other text first; and \f[CR]\-V\f[R] compares version
numbers.
At most one of \f[CR]\-g\f[R], \f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-M\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] applies to a key, and none combines with \f[CR]\-d\f[R],
\f[CR]\-i\f[R], or \f[CR]\-V\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]\-z\f[R] uses NUL line ends, \f[CR]\-u\f[R] keeps the first of
equal keys, \f[CR]\-c\f[R] reports the first unsorted line and
\f[CR]\-C\f[R] only the status, \f[CR]\-o\f[R] writes to a file,
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] skips the last\-resort whole\-line comparison, and
\f[CR]\-m\f[R] is accepted.
.SS \f[CR]split\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]split\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Split a file or standard input into pieces of 1000 lines, the count of
\f[CR]\-l\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-lines\f[R], or the byte count of
\f[CR]\-b\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-bytes\f[R], called xaa, xab, and so on, or
with the prefix operand.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-suffix\-length\f[R] sets the number of suffix
letters.
A count of 0 returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]stat\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]stat\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print file or file system metadata.
\f[CR]\-L\f[R], \f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-c\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\-printf\f[R] follow links, select the file system, select terse
output, apply a format, and apply a format without a trailing newline.
.SS \f[CR]strings\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]strings\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print each run of at least four printable characters in each file or
standard input.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-bytes\f[R] sets the minimum length,
\f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-radix\f[R] prefixes each string with its
offset in d, o, or x, and \f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-all\f[R] scans the
whole file.
.SS \f[CR]stty\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]stty\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Report or change terminal settings; \f[CR]\-a\f[R] reports all, and
\f[CR]\-g\f[R] prints a state that restores them later.
.SS \f[CR]sync\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]sync\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Flush file system data.
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] flushes the data of each operand, and \f[CR]\-f\f[R] the
file system of each operand, or every file system without one.
.SS \f[CR]tabs\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]tabs\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Set the terminal tab stops to every 8 columns, every N columns for \-N,
or the listed columns, by writing escape sequences to standard output.
.SS \f[CR]tee\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]tee\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Copy standard input to files, appending with \f[CR]\-a\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]timeout\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]timeout\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run a command with a time limit, with an optional duration suffix.
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-signal\f[R] selects the signal,
\f[CR]\-k\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-kill\-after\f[R] sends KILL after a further
duration, and \f[CR]\-p\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-preserve\-status\f[R] returns
the command status.
.SS \f[CR]touch\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]touch\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Update file times.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-m\f[R], \f[CR]\-r\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-t\f[R] select the access time, skip missing files, select the
modification time, copy a reference file, and set a timestamp.
.SS \f[CR]tput\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]tput\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Write the escape sequence of a terminal capability from a fixed table.
The capabilities are bel, blink, bold, civis, clear, cnorm, cub1, cud1,
cuf1, cuu1, dch1, dl1, ed, el, home, ich1, il1, ind, init, reset, rev,
rmso, rmul, sgr0, smso, smul, and cup with a row and a column.
cols and lines print the terminal size and need a terminal, and longname
prints the description of TERM or of \f[CR]\-T\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-terminal\f[R].
An unknown capability returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]tr\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]tr\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Translate bytes with ranges, classes, [=c=] equivalence classes, and
[c*n] repeats.
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] deletes, \f[CR]\-c\f[R] and \f[CR]\-C\f[R] complement the
first set, and \f[CR]\-s\f[R] squeezes runs of a byte from the last set.
A shorter second set is padded with its last byte unless \f[CR]\-t\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-truncate\-set1\f[R] truncates the first.
.PP
When translating, the second set accepts only [:upper:] and [:lower:],
each aligned with an [:upper:] or [:lower:] class in the first set, and
one [c*] repeat that fills it to the length of the first; a [c*] in the
first set is an error.
.SS \f[CR]tsort\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]tsort\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print a total order of the pairs of words in a file or standard input.
A cycle prints a partial order and returns 1.
.SS \f[CR]tty\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]tty\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the terminal of standard input, or not a tty with status 1.
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-silent\f[R] prints nothing.
.SS \f[CR]uname\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]uname\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the system name, or the fields of \f[CR]\-m\f[R] (machine),
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] (node), \f[CR]\-r\f[R] (release), \f[CR]\-s\f[R]
(system), and \f[CR]\-v\f[R] (version), or all of them with
\f[CR]\-a\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]uniq\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]uniq\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Collapse adjacent equal lines.
\f[CR]\-c\f[R] counts, \f[CR]\-d\f[R] and \f[CR]\-u\f[R] print only
repeated or only unique lines, and \f[CR]\-D\f[R] every line of a
repeated run.
\f[CR]\-f\f[R] and \f[CR]\-s\f[R] skip fields and characters,
\f[CR]\-w\f[R] compares at most N characters, and \f[CR]\-i\f[R] ignores
ASCII case.
\f[CR]\-\-all\-repeated\f[R][=\f[I]none\f[R]|\f[I]prepend\f[R]|\f[I]separate\f[R]]
acts as \f[CR]\-D\f[R] with empty lines before or between runs, and
\f[CR]\-\-group\f[R][=\f[I]separate\f[R]|\f[I]prepend\f[R]|\f[I]append\f[R]|\f[I]both\f[R]]
prints every line with empty lines between, before, or after runs and
excludes \f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-D\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-u\f[R].
\f[CR]\-z\f[R] uses NUL line ends, and a second operand is the output
file.
.SS \f[CR]unlink\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]unlink\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Remove one non\-directory path.
.SS \f[CR]watch\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]watch\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Run a command at an interval.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-g\f[R], and \f[CR]\-e\f[R] set
the interval, omit the title, exit when output changes, and exit after a
command error; \f[CR]\-x\f[R] is accepted.
On a terminal, q or an interrupt exits with status 0, and the command
reads cooked terminal input.
.SS \f[CR]wc\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]wc\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Count lines, words, characters, and bytes with \f[CR]\-l\f[R],
\f[CR]\-w\f[R], \f[CR]\-m\f[R], and \f[CR]\-c\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]which\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]which\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Resolve builtins, PATH programs, and bundled utilities, with
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-all\f[R] and \f[CR]\-q\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]who\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]who\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the logged\-in users with their terminals and login times.
The options \-a, \-b, \-d, \-l, \-p, \-r, \-t, and \-T select records
and fields as POSIX describes, \f[CR]\-H\f[R] adds headings,
\f[CR]\-m\f[R] and \f[CR]am i\f[R] select the terminal of standard
input, \f[CR]\-q\f[R] prints the users and their count, \f[CR]\-s\f[R]
the short form, and \f[CR]\-u\f[R] idle times.
.SS \f[CR]whoami\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]whoami\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Print the effective user.
.SS \f[CR]xargs\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]xargs\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Append arguments from standard input to a command, which reads the null
device.
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-arg\-file\f[R] reads arguments from a file and
leaves the command standard input.
\f[CR]\-0\f[R] splits on NUL and \f[CR]\-d\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-delimiter\f[R] on one character or escape such as \[rs]n,
\[rs]t, \[rs]x41, or \[rs]0; both keep quotes and backslashes literal
and disable the end marker.
.PP
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] skips an empty input, \f[CR]\-E\f[R] sets the end marker,
and \f[CR]\-e\f[R][\f[I]EOF\f[R]], \f[CR]\-\-eof\f[R][=\f[I]EOF\f[R]]
sets or removes it.
\f[CR]\-I\f[R] replaces a string with each input line, and
\f[CR]\-i\f[R][\f[I]R\f[R]], \f[CR]\-\-replace\f[R][=\f[I]R\f[R]] does
so with R or {}.
\f[CR]\-L\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R], and \f[CR]\-s\f[R] limit each command to
lines, arguments, or bytes; for \f[CR]\-L\f[R], empty lines do not count
and a trailing blank continues a line.
.PP
\f[CR]\-P\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-max\-procs\f[R] runs that many commands at
once, or as many as possible for 0, with output that may interleave.
\f[CR]\-p\f[R] asks on the terminal before each command, \f[CR]\-t\f[R]
traces, and \f[CR]\-x\f[R] fails when a command does not fit.
The status is 123 when a command fails, 124 when one exits with 255, 125
when a signal ends one, and 126 or 127 when one cannot run, which under
\f[CR]\-P\f[R] stops the run after the running commands finish.
.SS \f[CR]yes\f[R]
\f[B]\f[CB]yes\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Repeat the joined operands, or y, until output closes.
.SS Locale
The first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE, and LANG selects the
character locale.
A UTF\-8 or utf8 codeset selects UTF\-8 when the platform supports it,
and anything else selects bytes.
In UTF\-8, a ?
and each step of a * consume one character, a bracket expression
compares code points, ${#v} and ${v:offset:length} count characters, and
${v\[ha]}, ${v,}, and ${v\[ti]} change the case of multibyte letters.
.PP
This applies to pathname expansion, case, [[ == ]], ${v#pat}, ${v%pat},
${v/pat/rep}, ${v\[ha]pat}, and compgen \-X.
Completion always matches by UTF\-8 sequence.
.PP
In the Bash moods pathname expansion sorts by the collation order that
LC_ALL or LC_COLLATE selects.
The collation order is read from the environment the first time a
pattern is sorted.
The first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_NUMERIC, and LANG selects the
numeric locale of \f[CR]printf\f[R].
.SS Restricted shell
A restricted shell rejects directory changes, including those of pushd,
popd, and z, and changes to SHELL, PATH, ENV, and BASH_ENV, which become
read\-only.
It rejects a directory separator in a command word or a source operand,
the redirections >, >|, <>, >>, &>, &>>, and >&, \f[CR]exec\f[R],
\f[CR]newgrp\f[R], \f[CR]bench\f[R], \f[CR]assimilate\f[R], and
\f[CR]slurp\f[R], \f[CR]hash \-p\f[R], \f[CR]command \-p\f[R],
\f[CR]enable \-f\f[R], \f[CR]enable \-d\f[R], \f[CR]set \-M\f[R],
\f[CR]set \-I\f[R], \f[CR]set +I\f[R], \f[CR]set +r\f[R],
\f[CR]source \-\-mood\f[R], history file operands, every form of
\f[CR]koshkit\f[R], and the \f[CR]koshconf\f[R] forms \f[CR]create\f[R],
\f[CR]set\f[R], \f[CR]load\f[R], and \f[CR]save \-\-persist\f[R].
Numeric descriptor duplication stays available, and so does
\f[CR]set \-o posix\f[R].
.PP
A command word falls back only to a bundled utility that a startup file
enabled with \f[CR]enable\f[R].
After startup, \f[CR]enable\f[R] can disable a builtin but cannot enable
one or change a bundled utility.
An enabled utility that starts other programs, such as \f[CR]env\f[R],
\f[CR]xargs\f[R], \f[CR]find\f[R], \f[CR]nohup\f[R], \f[CR]timeout\f[R],
or \f[CR]watch\f[R], can start any program, including another shell, so
a startup file for a restricted shell should enable only utilities that
start none.
.PP
No setting turns restricted mode off.
A shell started restricted by \f[CR]\-r\f[R] or as rbash ignores and
removes \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] with a warning, and applies only
presentation and diagnostic options from the settings files.
It ignores the mood, a semantic option, editor.history.file_path, and
editor.history.max_entries there with a warning.
.PP
A child shell inherits the restrictions, and a sourced script keeps
them.
A shell script found through PATH and run as a command runs without
them.
.SS Related shells
The \f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]dash\f[R], \f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]fish\f[R],
and \f[CR]busybox\f[R] manuals describe their respective shells.
